.seo-faq-del:hover{color:var(--err-strong)}
.seo-tags{background:var(--card-grad);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius-l);box-shadow:var(--warm-shadow);padding:20px;margin-top:16px}
.seo-tags-head{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:12px}
.seo-tags-note{font-size:12.5px;color:var(--t2);line-height:1.5;margin:8px 0 12px}
.seo-tags-note code{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:11px;background:var(--bg-3);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:4px;padding:1px 5px;color:var(--t1)}
/* SOFT-WRAPS rather than scrolling — the "wrap first" branch of the rail
   doctrine (see `.rail-overflow`). `white-space:pre` + `overflow-x:auto` with no
   affordance sliced the output mid-attribute at 390px: the last visible line
   read `<meta property="og:type" content="website` with the closing quote and
   bracket over the edge, which does not read as "scroll me", it reads as
   corrupt output — on a panel whose whole job is to show the user correct
   markup they are about to copy. `pre-wrap` keeps every deliberate newline (one
   tag per line) while letting a long tag fold, and `overflow-wrap:anywhere`
   folds only where a line genuinely cannot fit, so short tags are untouched.
   This is what the two sibling code blocks in this stylesheet already do —
   `.bsg-code` and `.uib-code`. Wrapping needs no gesture and cannot hide a
   character. */
.seo-tags-code{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:12px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--t1);background:var(--bg-1);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:14px 16px;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-wrap:anywhere;margin:0}
