/* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   FOUNDRY — light-mode visual rebuild (Phase 1, structure-only)
   Additive design system + app shell. Prefixes: pnav- home- rail- soon- fx-
   ui-pill spec-frame. The dark theme + legacy tool CSS above is untouched;
   this layer restyles light and is what the rebuilt App renders.
   ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

/* ── Additive tokens ── */
:root{
  /* CATEGORY HUES. These were declared "theme-independent" and had no dark set,
     which is the bug: they are painted as 9-11px TEXT (.smap-soon, .smap-link-label,
     .hw-chrome-here, .home-foot-feat-title) as well as on icons. (.pnav-group-title
     was on this list and paints --t0; checked 2026-09-04.) Those text rules now
     read --hue-*-strong; what stays on --hue-* below is fills, dots and icons.
     and a saturated mid-tone that reads on paper is unreadable on #151619.
     Measured on the four dark grounds --bg-0..--bg-3 before the fix:
       --hue-colour     #2563EB  3.68 / 3.50 / 3.37 / 3.04
       --hue-type       #0F766E  3.47 / 3.31 / 3.18 / 2.88
       --hue-component  #7C3AED  3.34 / 3.17 / 3.05 / 2.76
       --hue-imagery    #B45309  3.78 / 3.60 / 3.47 / 3.13
       --hue-ai         #BE185D  3.15 / 3.00 / 2.88 / 2.61
       --hue-icons      #047857  3.47 / 3.30 / 3.17 / 2.87
     Every one under the 4.5:1 AA needs at those sizes; the worst was 2.61.
     The dark set below is the SAME hue and saturation with lightness raised
     until each clears 4.55 on --bg-3, the lightest ground it lands on.
     Two also missed in light, on the page ground #EFEEE9 specifically:
     --hue-colour was 4.45 and --hue-imagery 4.32, so both are a shade deeper
     here. The other four light values are untouched and measured >=4.71.
     If you add a category, measure it on --bg-3 AND on #EFEEE9, not on white.
     Pinned by tests/unit/category-hue-contrast.test.js. */
  --hue-colour:#205FEB;--hue-type:#0F766E;--hue-component:#7C3AED;--hue-imagery:#AD5009;--hue-ai:#BE185D;--hue-icons:#047857;
  /* THE READABLE COMPANION. --hue-* above is a FILL colour: dots, rails, icon
     tiles, borders, the OG cards. --hue-*-strong is the one that carries TEXT.

     They were one token, and that is why [hue-set-has-no-tint-margin] exists.
     A single value cannot be both the brand's category colour and an ink with
     room to sit on something: tuned to clear 4.5 on its BARE ground it lands at
     4.60 light / 4.56 dark, and any tint at all takes it under. Measured on the
     rendered page, a 12% tint of the hue's own colour under the page's hover
     film drops the light set to 3.63-3.95 and the dark set to 3.43-3.51.

     THE ITEM ASKED HOW MUCH MARGIN THE HUE SET SHOULD CARRY. The answer is
     none: a fill does not need margin, and buying margin by moving six
     brand-adjacent colours would change the Create categories, the mega-menu,
     the sitemap, the in-tool shell and every committed OG card to fix five text
     rules. The margin belongs to the text role, which is what --accent /
     --accent-strong already does here, and --ok / --ok-strong after it. This is
     the third time this sheet has reached for that split; it is the pattern.

     The note's own prescription -- 'target the bare grounds at 5.5-6:1 so a 12%
     self-tint still clears 4.5' -- does not hold, and it is worth recording:
     solved to 5.5 bare the set measures 4.04-4.55 on that tint, still short.
     These are solved the other way round, from the requirement inward.

     TARGET: 4.5:1 on an 18% tint of the BRAND hue over the worst ground, under
     the hover film. 18% because that is the largest tint this sheet actually
     paints (.hw-continue:hover); 12% and 14% and 10% and 7% all sit inside it.
     So a NEW surface can put hue text on any tint the codebase already uses and
     clear AA on arrival, which is the thing that was impossible before.
     Measured light, worst ground #EFEEE9 under the 4% --t0 hover film:
       --hue-colour-strong     #124CCA  bare 6.21  12% 4.89  18% 4.51
       --hue-type-strong       #0C5F58  bare 6.47  12% 5.13  18% 4.72
       --hue-component-strong  #6316E9  bare 6.41  12% 4.99  18% 4.59
       --hue-imagery-strong    #8D4107  bare 6.25  12% 4.94  18% 4.56
       --hue-ai-strong         #A31450  bare 6.52  12% 4.96  18% 4.51
       --hue-icons-strong      #036348  bare 6.27  12% 4.95  18% 4.55
     Same hue and saturation as the brand value, lightness only. Verify these by
     reading them off the rendered page, never by finding them in this file --
     the light tokens are declared twice, 5,200 lines apart, at equal
     specificity. Pinned by tests/unit/category-hue-contrast.test.js. */
  --hue-colour-strong:#124CCA;--hue-type-strong:#0C5F58;--hue-component-strong:#6316E9;
  --hue-imagery-strong:#8D4107;--hue-ai-strong:#A31450;--hue-icons-strong:#036348;
  /* Motion tokens (--dur-*, --ease-*, --t, --stagger) live in the first :root at the top of this file — do not redeclare them here. */
  --accent-active:#1E40AF;
  --s-11:160px;--s-12:192px;--sec-pad:clamp(96px,calc(8vw + 48px),160px);
  --fs-display:clamp(2.75rem,calc(1.8rem + 4vw),5rem);
  --fs-h1:clamp(2.25rem,calc(1.725rem + 2.2vw),3.5rem);
  --fs-h2:clamp(1.625rem,calc(1.36rem + 1.1vw),2.25rem);
  --fs-h3:1.5rem;--fs-h4:1.25rem;--fs-h5:1.0625rem;
  --fs-lede:clamp(1.0625rem,calc(0.96rem + 0.45vw),1.3125rem);
  --fs-body:1rem;--fs-body-s:0.875rem;--fs-caption:0.8125rem;--fs-micro:0.6875rem;
  --nav-h:60px;--nav-top:0px;--nav-clear:calc(var(--nav-top) + var(--nav-h) + 8px);
  /* Wide shared chrome: navigation, toolbars, and footers can use up to 1680px
     instead of collapsing to the old ~1400px desktop span. `--page-inline`
     is the actual viewport inset, so the content-max token means what it says.
     Every page container, tool toolbar, nav and footer reads --page-inline, so
     they share one horizontal alignment at every width.

     The centring branch is measured against `100vw`, NOT `100%`. A percentage
     inside a custom property is not resolved where the property is declared —
     it re-resolves against whichever element finally uses it. A full-bleed
     child sitting inside an already-padded page container therefore computed a
     *smaller* inset than its parent and bled the wrong distance. `100vw` is
     viewport-relative wherever it is read, so parent and child always agree.
     (100vw includes the classic scrollbar; that widens the inset by a few px
     above --page-content-max, symmetrically, for every consumer at once.) */
  --page-gutter:clamp(20px,1.5vw,32px);--page-content-max:1680px;
  --page-inline:max(var(--page-gutter),calc((100vw - var(--page-content-max))/2))
}

/* Large-desktop gutter step — founder direction, 2026-08-08. Below
   --page-content-max the centring branch has no slack to give, so a 1440-class
   screen fell back to the ~22px minimum gutter and the page ran flush to the
   viewport edge. This is the same shared scale one step wider, not a parallel
   one: nav, footer, page containers and tool toolbars all move together
   because they all read --page-inline. */
@media(min-width:1440px){:root{--page-gutter:clamp(36px,3.2vw,64px)}}

/* ── Foundry dark values ──
   THE CATEGORY HUES MUST BE RE-DECLARED HERE, NOT IN THE [data-theme="dark"]
   BLOCK AT THE TOP OF THE FILE. That block is EARLIER in source than the
   Foundry :root above, and `:root` and `[data-theme="dark"]` are both (0,1,0),
   so anything the Foundry :root declares silently beats it. That ordering is
   the entire reason these six were labelled "theme-independent" and shipped
   with no dark set at all — the author had nowhere to put one that worked.
   Verify a token here by reading it back off the rendered page in dark mode,
   never by finding the declaration in the sheet. */
[data-theme="dark"]{
  /* Same hue and saturation as light, lightness raised until each clears
     4.55:1 on --bg-3 #212327, the lightest ground they land on. Measured on
     --bg-0/--bg-1/--bg-2/--bg-3 after the change:
       --hue-colour     #5887F0  5.56 / 5.29 / 5.09 / 4.60
       --hue-type       #149C91  5.61 / 5.34 / 5.13 / 4.64
       --hue-component  #A070F2  5.52 / 5.25 / 5.05 / 4.57
       --hue-imagery    #DF670B  5.51 / 5.24 / 5.04 / 4.56
       --hue-ai         #E95391  5.53 / 5.26 / 5.06 / 4.58
       --hue-icons      #059E73  5.56 / 5.29 / 5.09 / 4.60 */
  --hue-colour:#5887F0;--hue-type:#149C91;--hue-component:#A070F2;
  --hue-imagery:#DF670B;--hue-ai:#E95391;--hue-icons:#059E73;
  /* The readable companion, dark. Same target as light: 4.5:1 on an 18% tint of
     the brand hue over the worst ground (--bg-3 #212327) under the 5% --t0
     hover film. Lighter than the brand value, because in dark the ink moves up.
       --hue-colour-strong     #85A8F4  bare 6.67  12% 4.99  18% 4.61
       --hue-type-strong       #19BEB0  bare 6.77  12% 5.12  18% 4.70
       --hue-component-strong  #BA98F5  bare 6.67  12% 5.01  18% 4.61
       --hue-imagery-strong    #F68A37  bare 6.45  12% 4.91  18% 4.58
       --hue-ai-strong         #EF85B1  bare 6.49  12% 4.93  18% 4.59
       --hue-icons-strong      #06BC88  bare 6.41  12% 4.84  18% 4.51
     THIS MUST STAY IN THIS BLOCK, not the [data-theme='dark'] block at the top
     of the file -- that one is EARLIER than the Foundry :root above and both
     are (0,1,0), so a dark value declared up there is silently dead. That trap
     is what shipped the hues with no dark set at all. */
  --hue-colour-strong:#85A8F4;--hue-type-strong:#19BEB0;--hue-component-strong:#BA98F5;
  --hue-imagery-strong:#F68A37;--hue-ai-strong:#EF85B1;--hue-icons-strong:#06BC88;
  /* STATE COLOURS, dark. Declared HERE rather than in the [data-theme="dark"]
     block at the top of the file for the reason #331 recorded the hard way:
     that block and the Foundry [data-theme="light"] block below are both
     (0,1,0), so whichever is later wins. Putting the dark set up top reads
     correctly in the sheet and paints the light values on the page.

     Dark is NOT the safe theme here, which is the assumption this pass was
     warned about. Two of the three roles need nothing — --ok #4ade80 and
     --warn #facc15 measure 9.03-12.41 bare and still hold 6.06 / 6.63 on their
     own 18% tint, so their -strong companions are the SAME VALUE, kept only so
     every call site can name one token and not branch per theme. --err is the
     exception: #f87171 is fine bare (5.69-6.87) and falls to 4.30 on an 18%
     --err tint over --bg-3, so it lifts to #F87B7B (4.60 on that same ground).

     --*-fg inverts here and that is the whole point of the token. White on
     these fills measures 1.74 (--ok), 1.53 (--warn) and 2.77 (--err) — the
     filled Approve and Delete buttons were painting white on a pale green and
     a pale red. Near-black gives 11.17 / 12.71 / 7.03. */
  --ok-strong:#4ade80;--warn-strong:#facc15;--err-strong:#F87B7B;
  --ok-fg:#0B0D10;--warn-fg:#0B0D10;--err-fg:#0B0D10
}

/* ── Foundry light values (overrides the earlier [data-theme="light"] block;
   same specificity, later wins — only Foundry-changed tokens are re-declared) ── */
[data-theme="light"]{
  /* V2: the page is a warm bone and cards RISE to white above it. That inverts
     how recession works here, so the ladder is not a straight grey ramp:
     --bg-2 sits BETWEEN bone and white (subtle fills, .btn:hover), while --bg-3
     and --bg-4 stay below the page for pressed/inset states. A darker-than-page
     text surface is what the V1 ramp had, and on a #EFEEE9 ground it cannot
     carry the V2 mute at AA — see the measurement below. */
  --bg-0:#EFEEE9;--bg-1:#FFFFFF;--bg-2:#F6F5F1;--bg-3:#E4E2DA;--bg-4:#D6D3C8;
  --card:#FFFFFF;--card-grad:#FFFFFF;--inp:#FFFFFF;--cbg:#F6F5F1;
  /* CONTRAST — re-measured for V2, 2026-08-16. The V2 page (#EFEEE9) is
     materially darker than the V1 page (#FAFAFA), so every ink figure had to be
     re-derived rather than carried over; the old note's numbers are now void.
     The spec's mute (#6C6C66) is anchored at --t3, the faintest step, and
     --t1/--t2 are warmed interpolations so the HIERARCHY the previous fix
     established survives. Measured on the three backgrounds each is actually
     used against (#FFFFFF / #EFEEE9 / #F6F5F1):
       --t1 #50504A → 8.12 / 6.99 / 7.44
       --t2 #5F5F59 → 6.43 / 5.53 / 5.89
       --t3 #6C6C66 → 5.28 / 4.55 / 4.84   (AA floor is 4.5 — --t3 on the page
                                            clears it by 0.05, so do not darken
                                            the page or lighten --t3 further)
       --t0 #0F0F10 → 19.16 / 16.49 / 17.56
     Dark theme is measured in the PR, not claimed here. */
  --t0:#0F0F10;--t1:#50504A;--t2:#5F5F59;--t3:#6C6C66;
  --border:#DAD8CF;--bh:#C0BDB0;--hvr:rgba(15,15,16,.04);--scrim:rgba(15,15,16,.32);
  /* ACCENT — founder-selected blue, 2026-08-16. The two tokens carry different
     jobs and are NOT interchangeable:
       --accent        #0F6FFF  3.82 / 4.43 / 4.06  (page / white / --bg-2)
       --accent-strong #0B5ED7  5.03 / 5.84 / 5.35
     --accent misses the 4.5:1 floor for NORMAL-size text on every ground. It is
     for fills, borders, icons, focus rings and LARGE display text (>=24px, or
     >=18.66px bold), where the floor is 3:1. Any accent-coloured text below that
     — and V2 leans on 11-12px mono eyebrows, counts and category labels — must
     use --accent-strong. This is the whole reason both tokens exist; do not
     collapse them. Spec: design-language-v2.md#the-blue-accent-is-not-safe-for-small-text
     --accent-soft #4D90FF is decorative only (2.68 on the page) — never text. */
  --accent:#0F6FFF;--accent-strong:#0B5ED7;--accent-soft:#4D90FF;--accent-bg:rgba(15,111,255,.08);--accent-glow:rgba(15,111,255,.14);
  --brand:#0F6FFF;--brand-soft:#4D90FF;--brand-bg:rgba(15,111,255,.08);--brand-glow:rgba(15,111,255,.12);--info:#0F6FFF;--info-bg:rgba(15,111,255,.08);
  /* Same value as dark — the highlight does not lighten. See the dark block. */
  --hi:#E9FF64;--hi-fg:#0F0F10;
  /* Default-avatar background: brand-blue → darker. References --brand/--accent-strong so it resolves per-theme at use-site. */
  --avatar-grad:linear-gradient(140deg,var(--brand),var(--accent-strong));
  /* STATE COLOURS — three tokens per role, and they are NOT interchangeable.
     These light values are already darker than the ones in the early
     [data-theme="light"] block at the top of this file; THIS block wins, because
     both selectors are (0,1,0) and this one is later. Anyone measuring --ok by
     reading that earlier block is measuring a dead value (#16a34a, 2.84:1) —
     the live one is below.

       --ok      #15803D  4.32 / 5.02 / 4.60 / 3.87   (--bg-0 / card / --bg-2 / --bg-3)
       --warn    #A16207  4.24 / 4.92 / 4.51 / 3.80
       --err     #DC2626  4.16 / 4.83 / 4.43 / 3.72

     Bare, each of those already clears 4.5 on the card. They do NOT clear it on
     the page ground, on --bg-3, or — the case that actually bites — on their own
     colour-mix tint, which is how this codebase paints nearly every state badge:
     a 16% --err tint drops --err to 3.76 on the card and 3.28 on the page.
     So the base token keeps the fills, borders, dots and glyph marks at the 3:1
     floor, and any state-coloured TEXT below 24px uses the -strong companion.
     Solved by walking lightness at fixed hue and saturation until every ground
     — bare and tinted 6-18% — cleared 4.5 with headroom, rather than by picking
     a shade index, which #342 recorded as an assumption about a ramp's shape:
       --ok-strong   #10622F  6.43 / 7.47 / 6.85 / 5.76  (worst tinted ground 4.62)
       --warn-strong #7B4B05  6.34 / 7.37 / 6.75 / 5.68  (worst 4.61)
       --err-strong  #A21A1A  6.70 / 7.78 / 7.13 / 6.00  (worst 4.62)

     --*-fg is the ink that sits ON a filled state control. White clears on all
     three light fills (5.02 / 4.92 / 4.83); the ONE that did not was
     .grg-queue-status, which hard-coded #0B0D10 on --warn for 3.95. The token
     exists so that ink flips with the theme — see the dark block, where white
     on --ok is 1.74:1. */
  --ok:#15803D;--warn:#A16207;--err:#DC2626;
  --ok-strong:#10622F;--warn-strong:#7B4B05;--err-strong:#A21A1A;
  --ok-fg:#FFFFFF;--warn-fg:#FFFFFF;--err-fg:#FFFFFF;
  --shadow-xs:0 1px 2px rgba(10,10,11,.04);
  --shadow-s:0 1px 2px rgba(10,10,11,.04),0 2px 8px rgba(10,10,11,.04);
  --shadow-m:0 2px 4px rgba(10,10,11,.03),0 12px 32px -8px rgba(10,10,11,.10);
  --shadow-l:0 4px 8px rgba(10,10,11,.03),0 32px 64px -16px rgba(10,10,11,.14);
  --shadow-nav:0 1px 2px rgba(10,10,11,.04),0 8px 32px -12px rgba(10,10,11,.12)
}

/* ══ prefers-contrast: more ═════════════════════════════════════
   Until 2026-09-04 this file contained ZERO prefers-contrast rules across
   11,000-plus lines. Rendered with the browser reporting prefers-contrast: more
   (matchMedia confirmed true in-run), every resolved token was identical to the
   default — --border #dad8cf, --t2 #5f5f59, --t3 #6c6c66. The preference was
   received and ignored. The @media(forced-colors:active) block further down is
   a different and much blunter mechanism (Windows High Contrast); it does
   nothing for the graded preference macOS Increase Contrast and Chrome expose.

   IT HAS TO SIT HERE, BELOW THE FOUNDRY BLOCKS. A media query adds no
   specificity: these selectors are (0,1,0) exactly like the [data-theme] blocks
   above and the two at the top of the file, so this wins only by being later.
   Placed above the Foundry light block it would read correctly and paint
   nothing — the same trap that made the first dark hue set dead.
   Pinned by tests/unit/prefers-contrast.test.js, which asserts the ORDERING as
   well as the numbers.

   THE TEXT ROLES STAY A LADDER. Solved naively — every role to 7:1 — --t1, --t2
   and --t3 all collapse to #50504B in light: one value, no hierarchy, on a
   preference that is about legibility, not about flattening the page. They are
   graded instead, 9 / 8 / 7, so the ladder survives and every step clears AAA.
   Worst ground per theme (#EFEEE9 light, #212327 dark):
     --t1  #50504A 6.99 → #3F3F3B 9.10   |  #BFBEB6 8.44 → #C5C4BD 9.00
     --t2  #5F5F59 5.53 → #474743 8.03   |  #9F9F98 5.91 → #BABAB5 8.08
     --t3  #6C6C66 4.55 → #4F4F4B 7.08   |  #8E8E88 4.78 → #AEAEA9 7.06

   BORDERS GO TO THE 1.4.11 FLOOR, which they are nowhere near by default:
   --border is 1.23:1 against the page in light and 1.11:1 in dark, i.e. a
   hairline that a user asking for more contrast cannot see at all. 3:1 is the
   non-text minimum; --bh goes to 4.5 so hover stays distinguishable from rest
   rather than collapsing onto it (at 3:1 both solve to the same value).
     --border #DAD8CF 1.23 → #8F896F 3.03  |  #2A2B2F 1.11 → #696C76 3.00
     --bh     #C0BDB0 1.62 → #716C5A 4.53  |  #3C3D42 1.45 → #878992 4.51

   THE ACCENT PAIR DELIBERATELY COLLAPSES HERE, and the backlog note's
   prescription for it was HALF WRONG. It said to swap --accent for
   --accent-strong. That is right in light (3.82 → 5.03) and BACKWARDS in dark,
   where --accent #6FA8FF is 6.54 and --accent-strong #4A90FF is 5.04 — the
   swap would have LOWERED contrast for the exact user asking for more. Both
   tokens are therefore pushed to a measured 7:1 per theme instead, which also
   lifts the fills, borders, icons and large display text --accent carries and
   which the swap would have left alone. The white/near-black mirror on the
   filled controls is checked, not assumed: #FFFFFF on #094AAA is 8.17, and
   #101012 on #7AAFFF is 8.51.
   Every figure here is measured on the ROUNDED hex, not on the float the solver
   walked — measuring before rounding overstated three of them by ~0.03 and put
   --t3 and --border a hair under their targets.

   NOT DONE HERE, on purpose. The --hue-* category set has almost no margin
   (worst 4.60 light / 4.56 dark on its BARE grounds — see the .smap-soon note)
   and is exactly what this preference should lift. It is left out because
   tests/unit/category-hue-contrast.test.js asserts there is exactly ONE dark
   block declaring --hue-colour, so adding a second is a deliberate change to
   that contract and belongs with the retune, not with this first pass.
   SCOPE: the graded preference only. Real assistive-technology testing and
   screen-reader announcement remain unrun — see [audit-coverage-not-run]. */
@media (prefers-contrast: more){
  [data-theme="light"]{
    --t1:#3F3F3B;--t2:#474743;--t3:#4F4F4B;
    --border:#8F896F;--bh:#716C5A;
    --accent:#094AAA;--accent-strong:#094AAA;--brand:#094AAA;--info:#094AAA
  }
  [data-theme="dark"]{
    --t1:#C5C4BD;--t2:#BABAB5;--t3:#AEAEA9;
    --border:#696C76;--bh:#878992;
    --accent:#7AAFFF;--accent-strong:#7AAFFF;--brand:#7AAFFF;--info:#7AAFFF
  }
}

/* ── Category hue mapping (data-hue → --hue; never an inline colour) ── */
/* Each category carries BOTH roles: --hue paints, --hue-strong reads. A rule
   that sets text colour wants --hue-strong; a rule that fills, outlines or
   draws an icon wants --hue. Where a rule has no [data-hue] ancestor both fall
   back to --accent-strong, which is the readable member of the accent pair --
   the fallbacks must agree, because a rule whose ink and ground name different
   tokens is a latent bug even when the number happens to pass (.smap-soon was
   exactly that: #0B5ED7 ink on a 12% tint of #0F6FFF). */
[data-hue="colour"]{--hue:var(--hue-colour);--hue-strong:var(--hue-colour-strong)}
[data-hue="type"]{--hue:var(--hue-type);--hue-strong:var(--hue-type-strong)}
[data-hue="component"]{--hue:var(--hue-component);--hue-strong:var(--hue-component-strong)}
[data-hue="imagery"]{--hue:var(--hue-imagery);--hue-strong:var(--hue-imagery-strong)}
[data-hue="ai"]{--hue:var(--hue-ai);--hue-strong:var(--hue-ai-strong)}
[data-hue="icons"]{--hue:var(--hue-icons);--hue-strong:var(--hue-icons-strong)}
/* The sixth category. The other five now carry AA-clearing, theme-aware
   values, so the accent one has to as well: --accent is 4.43 on the card and
   3.82 on the page ground, and --hue is painted as 9-11px text. */
[data-hue="accent"]{--hue:var(--accent-strong)}

/* ── Sitemap (/sitemap) — the full visual map of the product ──
   Each Create category carries data-hue, so its dot + Soon badge tint match the
   mega-menu and the in-tool shell. Non-hued sections fall back to --accent. */
.smap-surface{margin-top:64px}
.smap-surface:first-of-type{margin-top:48px}
.smap-surface-h{margin-bottom:24px;padding-bottom:16px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)}
.smap-surface-h h2{font-family:var(--display);font-size:var(--fs-h2);font-weight:500;letter-spacing:-.02em;color:var(--t0);margin:0 0 6px}
.smap-surface-h p{font-size:var(--fs-body-s);color:var(--t2);margin:0;max-width:60ch}
.smap-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(260px,1fr));gap:16px}
.smap-grid--two{grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(280px,1fr))}
.smap-cat{border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius);background:var(--card);padding:20px;transition:border-color var(--dur-3) var(--ease-out),box-shadow var(--dur-3) var(--ease-out)}
.smap-cat:hover{border-color:var(--bh);box-shadow:var(--shadow-s)}
.smap-cat-h{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;margin-bottom:6px}
.smap-cat-h h3{font-size:var(--fs-h5);font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-.01em;color:var(--t0);margin:0}
.smap-cat-dot{width:8px;height:8px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--hue,var(--accent));flex:0 0 auto}
.smap-cat-desc{font-size:var(--fs-caption);color:var(--t2);line-height:1.5;margin:0 0 14px}
.smap-links{list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px}
.smap-link{margin:0}
.smap-link-a{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr auto;align-items:baseline;gap:8px 12px;padding:8px 10px;margin:0 -10px;border-radius:calc(var(--radius) - 4px);text-decoration:none;transition:background var(--dur-2) var(--ease-out)}
.smap-link-a:hover{background:var(--hvr)}
.smap-link-a--soon{cursor:default}
.smap-link-a--soon:hover{background:transparent}
.smap-link-a.active .smap-link-label{color:var(--hue-strong,var(--accent-strong))}
.smap-link-label{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;font-size:var(--fs-body-s);font-weight:500;color:var(--t0)}
.smap-link-a:hover .smap-link-label{color:var(--hue-strong,var(--accent-strong))}
.smap-link-a--soon:hover .smap-link-label{color:var(--t0)}
.smap-link-route{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:var(--fs-micro);color:var(--t3);text-align:right;white-space:nowrap}
.smap-link-note{grid-column:1/-1;font-size:var(--fs-caption);color:var(--t2);line-height:1.45}
/* THIS PILL PAINTS ITS INK ON A TINT OF THE SAME COLOUR, which is the shape of
   defect [accent-tint-grounds-unmeasured] was filed for. Two things were wrong.

   (1) THE FALLBACKS DISAGREED. The ink fell back to --accent-strong and the tint
   to --accent. On .smap-soon--inline, which has no [data-hue] ancestor, that
   painted #0B5ED7 on a 12% tint of #0F6FFF — two different tokens in one pill.
   Both now read --accent-strong. A rule whose ink and ground disagree about
   which token they mean is a latent bug even when the number happens to pass.

   (2) 12% WAS TOO MUCH TINT TO CARRY THE INK. Measured on the rendered page
   before this change: 4.32:1 light (#0b5ed7 on #d4dfec, the inline pill on the
   page ground) and 4.34:1 dark (#a070f2 on #292437, --hue-component on a card).
   Both under the 4.5 that 11px/600 needs. At 7% they measure 4.56 and 4.64.

   THE BORDER IS DOING THE WORK THE TINT USED TO DO. Dropping 12% to 7% costs
   the pill its presence, so it gains a 28% hue border — a border is OUTSIDE the
   text's ground, so it restores the shape and the colour signal without adding
   one point of luminance behind the letters.

   THE 7% IS NO LONGER A CEILING, AND THAT IS THE CHANGE HERE. It was one
   because the ink and the tint were the same token: the --hue-* set cleared
   4.5 on its bare ground by hundredths, so any tint at all took it under.
   The ink now reads --hue-strong, which is solved against an 18% tint of the
   brand hue, so this pill would clear AA at any percentage the sheet uses.
   See [hue-set-has-no-tint-margin] and the token comment above the set.

   IT STAYS AT 7% ANYWAY, on evidence rather than arithmetic. Across Ditto,
   Gorgias, Height, Basecamp, Origin and Tana, not one paints coloured
   micro-text on a tint of its own colour: the colour goes in a dot, an icon
   tile or the ink, and the ground stays neutral. Linear goes further and
   prints 'Contrast has been adjusted' beside a user-picked status colour --
   the same split as --hue / --hue-strong, shipped as a product feature. A
   faint wash plus a 28% border is that pattern; a heavier tint would be the
   thing the reference set avoids. The tint and border read --hue, the ink
   reads --hue-strong, and both fall back to the accent member that matches. */
.smap-soon{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;font-size:var(--fs-micro);font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.03em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--hue-strong,var(--accent-strong));background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--hue,var(--accent)) 7%,transparent);border:1px solid color-mix(in srgb,var(--hue,var(--accent)) 28%,transparent);border-radius:999px;padding:1px 6px;line-height:1;flex:0 0 auto}
.smap-soon--inline{vertical-align:middle;margin:0 1px}
.smap-stage{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;font-size:var(--fs-micro);font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.03em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--ok-strong);background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--ok) 10%,transparent);border-radius:999px;padding:2px 7px;line-height:1;flex:0 0 auto}
@media(max-width:520px){
  .smap-link-a{grid-template-columns:1fr}
  .smap-link-route{text-align:left}
}

/* ── Motion utilities (fx-) ── */
.fx-dot{width:8px;height:8px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--hue,var(--accent));display:inline-block;flex:0 0 auto;transition:transform var(--dur-2) var(--ease-spring)}
.fx-underline{background-image:linear-gradient(currentColor,currentColor);background-size:0% 1px;background-position:0 100%;background-repeat:no-repeat;transition:background-size var(--dur-3) var(--ease-out)}
.fx-underline:hover,a:hover>.fx-underline{background-size:100% 1px}
.fx-lift{transition:transform var(--dur-3) var(--ease-out),box-shadow var(--dur-3) var(--ease-out),border-color var(--dur-3) var(--ease-out)}
.fx-lift:hover{transform:translateY(-4px);box-shadow:var(--shadow-m);border-color:var(--bh)}
@keyframes fx-rise{from{opacity:0;transform:translateY(16px)}to{opacity:1;transform:none}}
@keyframes fx-pop{from{opacity:0;transform:scale(.9)}to{opacity:1;transform:none}}
@keyframes fx-fade{from{opacity:0;transform:translateY(4px)}to{opacity:1;transform:none}}

/* Scroll reveal — hook adds .is-in once. Children cascade via nth-child delay. */
[data-reveal]{opacity:0;transform:translateY(16px);transition:opacity var(--dur-5) var(--ease-out),transform var(--dur-5) var(--ease-out)}
[data-reveal].is-in{opacity:1;transform:none}
.home-anim{animation-fill-mode:both}

/* Reduced motion: content must never be stuck hidden. Belt-and-suspenders on top
   of the global kill-switch (lines 7-8), which only zeroes durations. */
html[data-reduced-motion="true"] [data-reveal],
html[data-reduced-motion="true"] .home-anim{animation:none!important;opacity:1!important;transform:none!important}
@media(prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){
  html:not([data-reduced-motion="false"]) [data-reveal],
  html:not([data-reduced-motion="false"]) .home-anim{animation:none!important;opacity:1!important;transform:none!important}
}

/* Lenis smooth-scroll (home only). Lenis adds these classes to <html> while it is
   mounted and removes them on teardown, so every rule here is inert elsewhere. */
html.lenis,html.lenis body{height:auto}
/* Scoped `html.lenis`, NOT `.lenis.lenis-smooth`. `lenis-smooth` is on the root
   only while a Lenis smooth scroll is IN FLIGHT (lenis 1.3.25 dist/lenis.mjs:1027
   builds the class list from `isScrolling === "smooth"`), so the old selector was
   absent at rest and the rule never applied at the moment a key was pressed —
   `html{scroll-behavior:smooth}` above governed keyboard scrolling instead, which
   is the opposite of what this rule exists to do. `lenis` alone is present for as
   long as the instance is mounted, which is exactly "Lenis owns this scroller".
   Two engines must not animate one scroll: Lenis drives the wheel, so the browser
   is told to leave the root alone. Reduced motion is unaffected — Lenis is never
   instantiated there, so this never matches, and the clamp above already sets
   `scroll-behavior:auto`. */
html.lenis{scroll-behavior:auto!important}
/* Scroll containment for overlays. One change from the rule this replaces: it
   was scoped to `.lenis.lenis-smooth`, so it did nothing for a visitor with
   reduced motion on — Lenis is never instantiated for them, the class is never
   added, and their popups chained into the page natively instead. The bug this
   rule exists to stop was never Lenis-only.

   It stays on the marked element ALONE. Extending it to `[data-lenis-prevent] *`
   looks harmless — overscroll-behavior is documented as applying to scroll
   containers, and most of a dialog's descendants are not one — but it is not:
   measured 2026-09-03, `contain` on the plain <span> under the pointer stopped
