.cpk-pad{position:relative;width:100%;height:148px;border:0;background-image:linear-gradient(to top,#000,rgba(0,0,0,0)),linear-gradient(to right,#fff,rgba(255,255,255,0));cursor:crosshair;touch-action:none}
/* Inset, because a bled pad has no margin to paint an outline into. */
.cpk-pad:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--accent);outline-offset:-2px}
/* Big enough to find on a 148px pad, and legible over both ends of the value
   axis: a white ring for dark colours, a dark hairline outside it for light ones. */
.cpk-pad-thumb{position:absolute;width:18px;height:18px;margin:-9px 0 0 -9px;border-radius:50%;border:2px solid #fff;box-shadow:0 0 0 1px rgba(0,0,0,.4),0 1px 3px rgba(0,0,0,.3);pointer-events:none}
/* ── The hue slider, and why every rule here is descendant-qualified ────────
   `input[type="range"]` at the top of this file (~line 585) is the app's
   standard slider: `background:transparent`, `height:32px`, a 4px `--bg-4`
   runnable track and a 20px white-on-accent thumb. Its specificity is (0,1,1);
   a bare `.cpk-hue` is (0,1,0), so the STANDARD SLIDER WON — and had been
   winning since this component was written. The hue strip has never actually
   painted its rainbow: what shipped was a thin grey track with the same blue
   thumb as a stroke-width or a quality slider, on the one control in the app
   whose entire job is to show you the hue you are choosing.

