/* ── FLAIR PILL ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   10.5px BOLD text in --flair-c on a 12% tint of ITSELF, so every tone has to
   clear 4.5:1 against its own tint, not against the bare card.

   THE NAMED TONES USED TO BE RAW HEXES WITH NO DARK VALUES — the same defect
   class as the category hues, and left by #331 because .flair renders only on
   /settings and in signed-in community bylines, which a signed-out capture
   cannot reach. Measured before, on the tint each actually lands on:
     blue #2563EB 3.80 light / 2.75 dark   violet #7C3AED 4.14 / 2.54
     rose #E11D48 3.38 / 3.12              amber  #D97706 2.44 / 4.22
     green #15803D 3.71 / 2.82             slate  #64748B 5.49 / 2.92
     gold  #B7791F 2.78 / 3.72
   Eleven of the fourteen missed. Every value below is the SAME hue and
   saturation with lightness walked until the worst real ground clears 4.60.

   THE GROUNDS ARE MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED. A pill was injected into the real
   rendered /settings and /community and its composited ground read back:
   light #FFFFFF (rows, cards) and #EFEEE9 (page); dark #191A1D and #101012.
   #212327 is included as a margin for inset rows. Tuning against --bg-3/--bg-4
   as well would have forced amber to #864904 and gold to #795015, which is
   brown, not amber — the cost of guessing at a ground you have not measured.

   DO NOT MOVE THESE ONTO THE --hue-* TOKENS, which is what the backlog item
   suggested. That was written before [accent-tint-grounds-unmeasured] measured
   the hues themselves: --hue-component on its own 12% tint is 4.34:1, so
   inheriting them would have imported a failure rather than fixing one.

   Ordering: these are class selectors, and every dark value below is a
   [data-theme="dark"] descendant at (0,2,0), so it beats the (0,1,0) base
   wherever it sits in the file. That is why this block does NOT have to live
   below the Foundry :root the way the token blocks do — but it does anyway. */
.flair{--flair-c:var(--accent-strong);--flair-fg:#fff;display:inline-flex;align-items:center;flex:none;padding:1px 7px;border-radius:999px;font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.01em;line-height:1.5;white-space:nowrap;color:var(--flair-c);background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--flair-c) 12%,transparent);border:1px solid color-mix(in srgb,var(--flair-c) 30%,transparent)}
.flair--accent{--flair-c:var(--accent-strong)}
/* Light: worst-ground ratio on the pill's own 12% tint in the comment. */
.flair--blue{--flair-c:#1452DB}   /* 4.64 */
.flair--violet{--flair-c:#712AEC} /* 4.63 */
.flair--rose{--flair-c:#B7183B}   /* 4.63 */
.flair--amber{--flair-c:#914F04}  /* 4.60 */
.flair--green{--flair-c:#126E34}  /* 4.62 */
.flair--slate{--flair-c:#475569}  /* 5.49 — already passing, unchanged */
/* Earned badges get a warmer, higher-signal gold treatment. The gradient is
   why gold was INVISIBLE to the contrast walk: groundOf() bails on any
   background-image, so a gradient ground is skipped rather than measured, and
   gold scored nothing at all in both themes. Its stops are now expressed
   through --flair-c/--flair-warm so the dark override reaches them, and both
   ends are checked by hand in tests/unit/flair-tone-contrast.test.js.
   Light worst end 4.51; dark worst end 4.60. */
.flair--gold{--flair-c:#835616;--flair-warm:#D69E2E;background:linear-gradient(135deg,color-mix(in srgb,var(--flair-warm) 20%,transparent),color-mix(in srgb,var(--flair-c) 14%,transparent));border-color:color-mix(in srgb,var(--flair-c) 42%,transparent)}
/* Dark. Same hue and saturation, lightness raised until the worst ground
   (#212327) clears 4.60 on the pill's own 12% tint. */
[data-theme="dark"] .flair{--flair-fg:#0B0D10}
[data-theme="dark"] .flair--blue{--flair-c:#6F98F2}   /* 4.62 */
[data-theme="dark"] .flair--violet{--flair-c:#AE85F4} /* 4.63 */
[data-theme="dark"] .flair--rose{--flair-c:#ED718C}   /* 4.62 */
[data-theme="dark"] .flair--amber{--flair-c:#E57E06}  /* 4.62 */
[data-theme="dark"] .flair--green{--flair-c:#1DAF54}  /* 4.61 */
[data-theme="dark"] .flair--slate{--flair-c:#8E9BAE}  /* 4.60 */
[data-theme="dark"] .flair--gold{--flair-c:#E1A54D;--flair-warm:#E8BB79} /* 4.60 */

/* Flair picker (Settings › Account) --- */
.flairpick-hint{margin:0 0 10px;font-size:12px;color:var(--t3);line-height:1.5}
.flairpick-preview{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;padding:10px 12px;margin-bottom:14px;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius-s);background:var(--bg-2)}
.flairpick-preview-label{font-size:10px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--t3)}
.flairpick-group-label{margin:12px 0 8px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--t2)}
.flairpick-group-label:first-of-type{margin-top:0}
.flairpick-grid{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:7px}
/* Chips reuse the .flair pill look but as tappable targets; muted until picked.
   MUTED BY TINT, NOT BY OPACITY, and that is the whole point of this block.
   `opacity:.62` on the pill faded the LABEL as well as the fill, and because it
   fades ink and ground toward each other it cannot be compensated for by any
   choice of colour. Measured at .62 these chips carried their labels at 2.52:1
   in light and 2.80:1 in dark; even .82 only reached 3.68 and 3.92. These are
   real buttons a user picks their flair from, so 4.5 applies and no opacity
   short of 1.0 could reach it. The resting state now keeps the ink at full
   strength and mutes the SURFACE instead — half the tint and a lighter border
   — with hover restoring the full .flair treatment. Same visual intent,
   readable at every step. */
.flairpick-chip{padding:4px 11px;font-size:11.5px;cursor:pointer;background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--flair-c) 6%,transparent);border-color:color-mix(in srgb,var(--flair-c) 18%,transparent);transition:background var(--dur-2,.14s) var(--ease-out,ease),border-color var(--dur-2,.14s) var(--ease-out,ease),box-shadow var(--dur-2,.14s) var(--ease-out,ease),transform var(--dur-2,.14s) var(--ease-out,ease)}
.flairpick-chip:hover{background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--flair-c) 12%,transparent);border-color:color-mix(in srgb,var(--flair-c) 30%,transparent)}
/* THE SELECTED CHIP INVERTS, so its ink has to invert too. `color:#fff` was
   correct only for as long as the tones had no dark values: the dark tones are
   necessarily lighter, and white on them measures 2.81-2.87:1 across every
   tone. This is the same mirror defect the state tokens carry a --*-fg token
   for, and --flair-fg is that token: #fff in light (6.32-7.58), #0B0D10 in dark
   (6.78-6.91). Adding dark tones without this would have CREATED the bug. */
.flairpick-chip.is-on{color:var(--flair-fg);background:var(--flair-c);border-color:var(--flair-c);box-shadow:0 1px 4px color-mix(in srgb,var(--flair-c) 34%,transparent)}
.flairpick-chip:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--accent);outline-offset:2px}
/* Earned badges are not selectable, but they ARE meant to be read — the copy
   above them exists so people know the badges exist. So they are marked
   unavailable by a dashed, unfilled outline rather than dimmed to 50%, which
   would have made the one group nobody can act on the hardest group to read. */
.flairpick-chip.is-locked{cursor:default;background:transparent;border-style:dashed;border-color:color-mix(in srgb,var(--flair-c) 34%,transparent)}
.flairpick-chip.is-locked:hover{background:transparent;border-color:color-mix(in srgb,var(--flair-c) 34%,transparent)}

/* ── Gradient Generator (/color/gradient) ───────────────────────────────────
   The --ggn-* vars alias the app design tokens so the tool follows the active
   light/dark theme like every other tool page.

   SPLIT INTO TWO RULES 2026-09-05. The aliases and the PAGE LAYOUT used to
   share one declaration, which meant a second surface could not take the
   vocabulary without also taking `max-width:1120px` and 56px of page padding.
   The homepage workbench's Gradient mode now renders `.ggn-stop`, `.ggn-field`,
   `.ggn-label`, `.ggn-dial`, `.ggn-angle-num`, `.ggn-copy` and `.ggn-css` for
   real - the founder's complaint was that the mini tools share nothing with the
   tools they open - so `.hw-ggn` joins the alias rule and takes none of the
   layout. Nothing about `.ggn` itself changed; the declarations were only
   separated along the line between "what these tokens mean" and "where this
   page sits". */
.ggn,.hw-ggn{--ggn-accent:var(--accent);--ggn-accent-text:var(--accent-strong);--ggn-bg:var(--inp);--ggn-panel:var(--card);--ggn-panel-2:var(--bg-2);--ggn-line:var(--border);--ggn-line-2:var(--bh);--ggn-t0:var(--t0);--ggn-t1:var(--t1);--ggn-t2:var(--t2);color:var(--ggn-t0);font-family:var(--font)}
.ggn{max-width:1120px;margin:0 auto;padding:28px 24px 56px;min-height:100%}
.ggn *,.hw-ggn *{box-sizing:border-box}
/* Header */
.ggn-head{display:flex;align-items:flex-start;justify-content:space-between;gap:24px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:24px}
.ggn-eyebrow{display:block;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--ggn-accent-text);margin-bottom:10px}
.ggn-title-row{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:12px}
.ggn-title{font-size:30px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.02em;color:var(--ggn-t0);line-height:1.1;margin:0}
.ggn-badge{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;font-size:12px;font-weight:600;color:var(--ggn-accent-text);background:var(--accent-bg);border:1px solid color-mix(in srgb,var(--accent) 34%,transparent);padding:3px 11px;border-radius:var(--radius-pill);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
.ggn-sub{margin:10px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.55;color:var(--ggn-t2);max-width:520px}
.ggn-lock-note{display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:7px;margin:12px 0 0;padding:9px 13px;font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5;font-weight:500;color:var(--ggn-accent-text);background:var(--accent-bg);border:1px solid color-mix(in srgb,var(--accent) 30%,transparent);border-radius:var(--radius-s);max-width:520px}
.ggn-lock-note svg{flex-shrink:0;margin-top:2px}
.ggn-head-actions{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;flex-shrink:0}
.ggn-btn{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;font-family:var(--font);font-size:13px;font-weight:600;cursor:pointer;border-radius:var(--radius-l);padding:9px 18px;transition:all var(--t);white-space:nowrap;border:1px solid transparent}
/* Fills with --accent-strong, not --ggn-accent. White on #0F6FFF is 4.43:1 and
   the label is 12.5px; on --accent-strong it is 5.84 (light) / 6.09 (dark).
   This rule already reached --accent-strong on HOVER, so the RESTING state was
   the unreadable one — the same shape of bug .ui-pill-accent carried. */
.ggn-btn-accent{background:var(--accent-strong);border-color:var(--accent-strong);color:var(--accent-fg)}
.ggn-btn-accent:hover{background:var(--accent-strong);border-color:var(--accent-strong)}
.ggn-btn-ghost{background:transparent;border-color:var(--ggn-line-2);color:var(--ggn-t1)}
.ggn-btn-ghost:hover{border-color:var(--ggn-t2);color:var(--ggn-t0);background:var(--hvr)}
.ggn-btn-sm{padding:6px 13px;font-size:12px}
/* Disabled ("From palette" with <2 palette colours): dim, no pointer, and
   suppress the ghost hover so it reads as inert. Uses [aria-disabled] (not
   :disabled) so the button stays focusable + announced; the hover reset is
   scoped to the ghost variant so it can't leak onto other disabled .ggn-btn. */
.ggn-btn[aria-disabled="true"]{opacity:.45;cursor:not-allowed}
.ggn-btn-ghost[aria-disabled="true"]:hover{background:transparent;border-color:var(--ggn-line-2);color:var(--ggn-t1)}
/* Grid: preview + panel */
.ggn-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1.55fr) minmax(300px,1fr);gap:20px;align-items:start;margin-bottom:20px}
.ggn-preview-wrap{position:relative}
.ggn-preview{position:relative;aspect-ratio:16/11;border-radius:var(--radius-l);overflow:hidden;border:1px solid var(--ggn-line);box-shadow:var(--shadow-m)}
.ggn-preview-pills{position:absolute;top:14px;left:14px;display:flex;gap:8px;z-index:2}
.ggn-pill{font-size:11px;font-weight:600;color:#fff;background:rgba(10,11,13,.5);backdrop-filter:blur(8px);-webkit-backdrop-filter:blur(8px);border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.18);padding:5px 11px;border-radius:var(--radius-pill)}
.ggn-preview-hint{margin:9px 2px 0;font-size:12px;line-height:1.4;color:var(--ggn-t2)}
/* Stops bar overlaid at the bottom of the preview */
/* touch-action:none is load-bearing, not tidiness: without it a touch drag on
   the rail is claimed by the page scroller and the stop never moves. */
.ggn-bar{position:absolute;left:16px;right:16px;bottom:16px;height:26px;z-index:2;cursor:copy;touch-action:none;-webkit-user-select:none;user-select:none}
.ggn-bar-track{position:absolute;inset:0;border-radius:var(--radius-pill);border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.28);box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,.4)}
.ggn-handle{position:absolute;top:50%;width:20px;height:20px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--h-color,#fff);border:2px solid #fff;transform:translate(-50%,-50%);cursor:grab;padding:0;box-shadow:0 1px 4px rgba(0,0,0,.5);touch-action:none;transition:box-shadow var(--t),transform var(--t)}
.ggn-handle:hover{transform:translate(-50%,-50%) scale(1.12)}
.ggn-handle.is-active{box-shadow:0 0 0 3px rgba(255,255,255,.35),0 1px 4px rgba(0,0,0,.5)}
.ggn-handle:active,.ggn-handle.is-dragging{cursor:grabbing}
/* A handle being dragged is lifted above its neighbours and held enlarged, so
   it stays legible under the finger for the whole gesture. */
.ggn-handle.is-dragging{z-index:3;transform:translate(-50%,-50%) scale(1.2);box-shadow:0 0 0 3px rgba(255,255,255,.55),0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,.55)}
/* Live position readout — only while dragging or keyboard-focused. */
.ggn-handle-val{position:absolute;bottom:calc(100% + 8px);left:50%;transform:translateX(-50%);padding:3px 7px;border-radius:var(--radius-xs);background:rgba(10,11,13,.86);border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.22);color:#fff;font-family:var(--mono);font-size:var(--fs-micro);font-weight:600;line-height:1;white-space:nowrap;opacity:0;visibility:hidden;pointer-events:none;transition:opacity var(--dur-1) var(--ease-standard),visibility 0s linear var(--dur-1)}
.ggn-handle.is-dragging .ggn-handle-val,.ggn-handle:focus-visible .ggn-handle-val{opacity:1;visibility:visible;transition-delay:0s}
/* Control panel */
.ggn-panel{background:var(--ggn-panel);border:1px solid var(--ggn-line);border-radius:var(--radius-l);padding:18px}
.ggn-field{margin-bottom:18px}
.ggn-field:last-child{margin-bottom:0}
.ggn-label{display:block;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--ggn-t2);margin-bottom:9px}
.ggn-label-row{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;margin-bottom:9px}
.ggn-label-row .ggn-label{margin-bottom:0}
/* Segmented type control */
.ggn-seg{display:flex;gap:4px;background:var(--ggn-panel-2);border:1px solid var(--ggn-line);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:4px}
.ggn-seg-btn{flex:1;font-family:var(--font);font-size:12.5px;font-weight:600;color:var(--ggn-t1);background:transparent;border:none;border-radius:var(--radius-s);padding:8px 6px;cursor:pointer;transition:all var(--t)}
.ggn-seg-btn:hover{color:var(--ggn-t0)}
.ggn-seg-btn.is-on{background:var(--accent-strong);color:var(--accent-fg)}
/* Angle: dial + numeric entry (the dial is the only pointer affordance) */
.ggn-angle{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:16px}
.ggn-angle.is-disabled{opacity:.4;pointer-events:none}
.ggn-dial{position:relative;width:64px;height:64px;flex-shrink:0;border-radius:50%;background:var(--ggn-panel-2);border:1px solid var(--ggn-line-2);cursor:grab;touch-action:none}
.ggn-dial:active{cursor:grabbing}
.ggn-dial-hand{position:absolute;left:calc(50% - 1px);bottom:50%;width:2px;height:26px;background:var(--ggn-accent);border-radius:2px;transform-origin:center bottom;transform:rotate(0deg)}
.ggn-dial-center{position:absolute;left:50%;top:50%;width:8px;height:8px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--ggn-accent);transform:translate(-50%,-50%)}
/* No margin-top here: the old 7px cleared the removed range rail, so the numeric
   field now sits centred against the dial instead of hanging below its midline. */
.ggn-angle-ctrl{flex:1;min-width:0;display:flex;align-items:center}
.ggn-angle-val{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:12px;color:var(--ggn-t1);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
/* Editable numeric angle field — type an exact degree alongside the dial */
.ggn-angle-num{display:inline-flex;align-items:baseline;gap:1px;padding:2px 4px 2px 8px;background:var(--ggn-panel-2);border:1px solid var(--ggn-line);border-radius:var(--radius-s);transition:border-color var(--t)}
.ggn-angle-num:focus-within{border-color:var(--ggn-accent)}
/* 58px is wide enough for a 3-digit angle — with the rail gone this field is the
   only numeric readout in the Angle row, so "215" / "360" must never clip. */
.ggn-angle-input{width:58px;font-family:var(--mono);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;color:var(--ggn-t0);background:transparent;border:none;outline:none;text-align:right;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;-moz-appearance:textfield}
.ggn-angle-input::-webkit-outer-spin-button,.ggn-angle-input::-webkit-inner-spin-button{-webkit-appearance:none;margin:0}
.ggn-angle-deg{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:12px;color:var(--ggn-t2)}
/* CSS output */
.ggn-copy{font-family:var(--font);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;color:var(--ggn-accent-text);background:transparent;border:none;cursor:pointer;padding:0;transition:opacity var(--t)}
.ggn-copy:hover{opacity:.75}
.ggn-css{display:block;width:100%;text-align:left;background:var(--ggn-panel-2);border:1px solid var(--ggn-line);border-radius:var(--radius-s);padding:12px 14px;cursor:pointer;transition:border-color var(--t)}
.ggn-css:hover{border-color:var(--ggn-line-2)}
.ggn-css code{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:11.5px;line-height:1.5;color:var(--ggn-t1);word-break:break-all;white-space:pre-wrap}
.ggn-fmt{display:inline-flex;gap:3px;background:var(--ggn-panel-2);border:1px solid var(--ggn-line);border-radius:var(--radius-s);padding:3px;margin-bottom:9px}
.ggn-fmt-btn{font-family:var(--font);font-size:11px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.02em;color:var(--ggn-t2);background:transparent;border:none;border-radius:var(--radius-xs);padding:5px 12px;cursor:pointer;transition:all var(--t)}
.ggn-fmt-btn:hover{color:var(--ggn-t0)}
.ggn-fmt-btn.is-on{background:var(--accent-strong);color:var(--accent-fg)}
.ggn-fmt-btn:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--ggn-accent);outline-offset:2px}
/* The multi-line SVG export used to be capped at 190px with its own scroller.
   The inspector body now scrolls as one region, so the block flows at full height
   — no nested scroller, no clipped code. */
.ggn-css--block code{white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word}
/* Blocks (Stops / Presets) */
.ggn-block{background:var(--ggn-panel);border:1px solid var(--ggn-line);border-radius:var(--radius-l);padding:18px;margin-top:16px}
.ggn-block-head{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;margin-bottom:14px}
.ggn-block-head .ggn-label{margin-bottom:0}
.ggn-block-actions{display:flex;gap:8px}
/* Stop rows */
.ggn-stops{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:8px}
.ggn-stop{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;background:var(--ggn-panel-2);border:1px solid var(--ggn-line);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:8px 10px;cursor:pointer;transition:border-color var(--t)}
.ggn-stop:hover{border-color:var(--ggn-line-2)}
.ggn-stop.is-active{border-color:var(--ggn-accent)}
.ggn-stop-idx{flex-shrink:0;width:16px;font-family:var(--mono);font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:var(--ggn-t2);text-align:center;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;user-select:none}
.ggn-stop-swatch{position:relative;flex-shrink:0}
.ggn-stop-swatch .cpk-pop{left:0;right:auto}
.ggn-stop-hex{flex:1;min-width:0;font-family:var(--mono);font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:var(--ggn-t0);background:transparent;border:none;outline:none;letter-spacing:.02em}
/* N2 (responsive-audit-2026-08): the stop's hex input clipped its own value at
   320–349px and again at 769–799px, rendering "#7C3AED" as "#7C3AE" — a
   plausible but wrong colour.

   The audit reads this as a width problem ("needs 91px and gets 77–87px"). It is
   not. The rule ABOVE never applied its font or its padding: `.ggn-stop-hex` is
   (0,1,0) and the global `input[type="text"],…` rule is (0,1,1), so the field
   was rendering in Manrope with the global form padding of 9px/14px — and at
   ≤640px in Manrope at 16px with 11px/14px — instead of the mono 13px it asks
   for. Measured at 320px: 103px of text in a 78px box. Its own sibling
   `.ggn-stop-pos input` is written as class+element, ties that specificity, wins
   on source order and has always rendered correctly, which is the tell.

   Raising the selector to `input.ggn-stop-hex` ties (0,1,1) and wins on source
   order. Measured after: scrollWidth equals clientWidth at 320, 769 and 900.

   `font-size` is deliberately NOT restated here. The 16px at ≤640px comes from
   the shared Forms / inputs block and exists to stop iOS zooming the page on
   focus; mono at 16px with no padding fits the box (78px of 78px), so the
   protection is kept rather than traded away for the designed 13px. */
input.ggn-stop-hex{font-family:var(--mono);padding:0}
.ggn-stop-pos{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:2px;flex-shrink:0}
.ggn-stop-pos input{width:46px;font-family:var(--mono);font-size:13px;color:var(--ggn-t1);background:var(--ggn-bg);border:1px solid var(--ggn-line);border-radius:var(--radius-xs);padding:5px 6px;outline:none;text-align:right;-moz-appearance:textfield}
.ggn-stop-pos input::-webkit-outer-spin-button,.ggn-stop-pos input::-webkit-inner-spin-button{-webkit-appearance:none;margin:0}
.ggn-stop-pos input:focus{border-color:var(--ggn-accent)}
.ggn-stop-pos span{font-size:12px;color:var(--ggn-t2)}
.ggn-stop-x{flex-shrink:0;width:26px;height:26px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-size:17px;line-height:1;color:var(--ggn-t2);background:transparent;border:1px solid var(--ggn-line);border-radius:var(--radius-s);cursor:pointer;transition:all var(--t)}
.ggn-stop-x:hover:not(:disabled){color:var(--err-strong);border-color:color-mix(in srgb,var(--err) 40%,transparent)}
.ggn-stop-x:disabled{opacity:.3;cursor:not-allowed}
/* Randomise locks — pin a setting so a shuffle keeps it */
.ggn-lock{flex-shrink:0;width:26px;height:26px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;color:var(--ggn-t2);background:transparent;border:1px solid var(--ggn-line);border-radius:var(--radius-s);cursor:pointer;transition:all var(--t)}
.ggn-lock:hover{color:var(--ggn-t0);border-color:var(--ggn-line-2)}
.ggn-lock.is-on{color:var(--accent-fg);background:var(--accent-strong);border-color:var(--accent-strong)}
.ggn-lock:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--ggn-accent);outline-offset:2px}
.ggn-stop-lock{width:26px;height:26px}
/* Locked stop handle on the preview bar — a solid ring marks it pinned */
.ggn-handle.is-locked{box-shadow:0 0 0 2px var(--accent),0 1px 4px rgba(0,0,0,.5)}
.ggn-handle.is-locked.is-active{box-shadow:0 0 0 2px var(--accent),0 0 0 4px rgba(255,255,255,.35),0 1px 4px rgba(0,0,0,.5)}
/* Presets grid (4×2) */
.ggn-presets{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr);gap:10px}
.ggn-preset{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:8px;background:transparent;border:none;padding:0;cursor:pointer;font-family:var(--font)}
.ggn-preset-swatch{display:block;width:100%;aspect-ratio:16/10;border-radius:var(--radius);border:1px solid var(--ggn-line);transition:transform var(--t),box-shadow var(--t)}
.ggn-preset:hover .ggn-preset-swatch{transform:translateY(-2px);box-shadow:var(--shadow-m)}
.ggn-preset-name{font-size:12px;font-weight:600;color:var(--ggn-t1);text-align:left}
.ggn-preset:hover .ggn-preset-name{color:var(--ggn-t0)}
/* Presets derived from the user's own palettes — an accent ring + tinted name
   set them apart from the built-in starter gradients in the same grid. */
.ggn-preset--mine .ggn-preset-swatch{border-color:var(--ggn-accent);box-shadow:0 0 0 1px var(--ggn-accent)}
.ggn-preset--mine .ggn-preset-name{color:var(--ggn-accent-text)}
@media(max-width:860px){
  .ggn-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}
  .ggn-presets{grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr)}
  .ggn-title{font-size:25px}
  .ggn-head-actions{width:100%}
  .ggn-btn{flex:1;justify-content:center}
}
@media(max-width:480px){
  .ggn{padding:20px 14px 44px}
  /* Three header buttons can't share one row on narrow phones (their nowrap
     labels overflow below ~380px) — wrap to 2-per-row + a full-width third. */
  .ggn-head-actions{flex-wrap:wrap}
  .ggn-btn{flex:1 1 calc(50% - 5px)}
  .ggn-stop{gap:6px;padding:8px}
  .ggn-stop-hex{font-size:12px;letter-spacing:0}
  .ggn-stop-pos input{width:40px;padding:5px 4px}
  .ggn-imports{grid-template-columns:1fr}
}
/* Generator: import-colours block (palette / saved projects) */
.ggn-imports{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(220px,1fr));gap:10px}
.ggn-import{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:12px;background:var(--ggn-panel-2);border:1px solid var(--ggn-line);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:10px 12px;cursor:pointer;font-family:var(--font);text-align:left;transition:border-color var(--t),transform var(--t)}
.ggn-import:hover{border-color:var(--ggn-accent);transform:translateY(-1px)}
.ggn-import-stripes{display:flex;flex-shrink:0;width:64px;height:36px;border-radius:var(--radius-s);overflow:hidden;border:1px solid var(--ggn-line)}
.ggn-import-stripes span{flex:1}
.ggn-import-id{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;min-width:0}
.ggn-import-name{font-size:12.5px;font-weight:600;color:var(--ggn-t0);white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis}
.ggn-import-meta{font-size:11px;color:var(--ggn-t2);white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis}
.ggn-import-empty{margin:0;font-size:13px;line-height:1.55;color:var(--ggn-t2)}
.ggn-import-empty a{color:var(--ggn-accent-text);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none}
.ggn-import-empty a:hover{text-decoration:underline}
/* Generator: gallery link in the Presets head */
.ggn-gal-link{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:4px;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;color:var(--ggn-accent-text);text-decoration:none;transition:opacity var(--t)}
.ggn-gal-link:hover{opacity:.75}

/* Gradient workbench hierarchy */
.ggn{max-width:1240px;padding-top:var(--s-7)}
.ggn-head{align-items:flex-end;margin-bottom:var(--s-5)}
.ggn-head-id{max-width:680px}
.ggn-title{font-size:clamp(38px,5vw,64px);font-weight:600;line-height:1.02;letter-spacing:-.04em}
.ggn-sub{max-width:650px;font-size:15px}
.ggn-head-actions{flex-wrap:wrap;justify-content:flex-end}
.ggn-btn{min-height:42px;border-radius:var(--radius-s)}
.ggn-btn:disabled{opacity:.45;cursor:not-allowed}
.ggn-status{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,minmax(0,1fr));margin-bottom:var(--s-5);border-block:1px solid var(--ggn-line)}
.ggn-status span{display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:6px;padding:12px 16px;font-size:11px;color:var(--ggn-t2)}
.ggn-status span+span{border-left:1px solid var(--ggn-line)}
.ggn-status strong{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:12px;color:var(--ggn-t0)}
.ggn-grid{grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1.65fr) minmax(330px,.85fr);gap:var(--s-5)}
.ggn-stage,.ggn-panel,.ggn-block{background:var(--ggn-panel);border:1px solid var(--ggn-line);border-radius:var(--radius-l)}
.ggn-stage{min-width:0;padding:12px}
.ggn-stage-head,.ggn-panel-head{display:flex;align-items:flex-start;justify-content:space-between;gap:var(--s-4);padding:8px 8px var(--s-4)}
.ggn-stage-head h2,.ggn-panel-head h2,.ggn-block-head h2{margin:3px 0 0;font-size:16px;font-weight:650;letter-spacing:-.015em;color:var(--ggn-t0)}
.ggn-step{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:9px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--ggn-accent-text)}
.ggn-preview{aspect-ratio:16/10;border-radius:var(--radius)}
.ggn-preview-wrap{min-width:0}
.ggn-bar{position:relative;inset:auto;height:46px;margin:12px 8px 0;padding:10px 0;cursor:copy}
.ggn-bar-track{inset:10px 0;height:26px}
.ggn-handle{top:50%}
.ggn-preview-hint{margin:5px 8px 2px}
/* Inspector height cap (two-column only). The slot is a normal grid item that
   stretches to the row height — which the canvas column alone sets, because the
   panel inside it is out of flow and contributes nothing to that row. The panel
   then fills the slot exactly, so it can never push the row taller and its body
   scrolls instead of clipping. Both are unset at 980px / 860px below. */
.ggn-panel-slot{position:relative;align-self:stretch;min-width:0}
.ggn-panel{position:absolute;inset:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;padding:12px 18px 18px}
.ggn-panel-head{flex-shrink:0;margin:0 -6px var(--s-4);border-bottom:1px solid var(--ggn-line)}
/* Negative margin lets the scrollbar sit in the panel's right padding while
   padding-right keeps the fields clear of it. */
.ggn-panel-body{flex:1 1 auto;min-height:0;overflow-y:auto;overscroll-behavior:contain;scrollbar-width:thin;padding-right:var(--s-1);margin-right:calc(var(--s-1) * -1)}
.ggn-panel-body:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--ggn-accent);outline-offset:2px}
.ggn-field{margin-bottom:var(--s-5)}
.ggn-seg-btn,.ggn-fmt-btn{min-height:36px}
.ggn-css{min-height:76px}
.ggn-block{padding:var(--s-5);margin-top:var(--s-4)}
.ggn-block-head{align-items:flex-start;gap:var(--s-4)}
.ggn-block-head p{margin:4px 0 0;font-size:11px;line-height:1.45;color:var(--ggn-t2)}
.ggn-stops{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:8px}
.ggn-stop{min-width:0}
.ggn-stop-hex-field{position:relative;display:flex;flex:1;min-width:0;flex-direction:column}
.ggn-stop-hex[aria-invalid="true"]{color:var(--err-strong)}
.ggn-field-error{position:absolute;top:calc(100% + 8px);left:-8px;z-index:5;min-width:max-content;padding:6px 8px;font-size:10px;color:var(--err-strong);background:var(--ggn-panel);border:1px solid color-mix(in srgb,var(--err) 38%,var(--ggn-line));border-radius:var(--radius-xs);box-shadow:var(--shadow-s)}
.ggn-starting{margin-top:var(--s-5)}
/* `.ggn-sources` retired (#323): the palette-import block and the curated rail
   were two cards making the same offer, and the first was numbered as a third
   step below the canvas. They are one `.ggn-starting` section now, with each
   source group labelled inside it. */
.ggn-start-group+.ggn-start-group{margin-top:var(--s-5)}
.ggn-start-label{display:block;margin-bottom:10px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--ggn-t2)}
.ggn-starting .ggn-import-empty{margin-top:var(--s-4)}
/* overflow / scrollbar / snap-type / edge fade come from `.rail-overflow`, which
   is on the element in the markup. */
.ggn-presets{display:grid;grid-auto-flow:column;grid-auto-columns:minmax(142px,1fr);grid-template-columns:none;padding-bottom:8px}
.ggn-preset{scroll-snap-align:start}
/* Reduced motion: drop the preset rail's scroll snapping. Unlike every other
   rail in this stylesheet this one is NOT scoped to a phone width — the strip
   is a single-row `grid-auto-flow:column` of 16 presets, so it overflows at
   every width measured (2196px of overflow at 320, still 1280px at 1440). The
   snap pull is +74px at all of them: scrollLeft set to 78px settles at 152px,
   and stays at 78px with snapping off. See the note on
   `.plb-toolbar-group:last-child` for why the global clamp does not cover this. */
html[data-reduced-motion="true"] .ggn-presets{scroll-snap-type:none}
@media(prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){
  html:not([data-reduced-motion="false"]) .ggn-presets{scroll-snap-type:none}
}
.ggn-preset-swatch{aspect-ratio:16/9}
.ggn-preset:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--ggn-accent);outline-offset:3px}
/* THE PEEK, and the one width where the rail needed one. A fixed 142px tile
   tiles to a fraction at every measured width — 7.51 tiles at 1440, 4.58 at
   834, 1.49 at 320 — so the third tile always half-shows and the rail reads as
   a rail. At ~390px it lands on almost exactly two: the box is 296px and two
   142px tiles plus their 10px gap are 294px, so the strip filled the card
   interior to within 2px and 14 of the 16 presets looked like they did not
   exist. Sizing the column as a FRACTION of the box instead of a fixed width
   removes the coincidence by construction: 2.4 tiles fit, so the third is
   always ~46px into view and always cut by the fade. Scoped to the one band
   that needs it — above 480px the fixed 142px already tiles to a fraction, and
   it keeps preset tiles a consistent size across the widths where they fit. */
@media(max-width:480px){
  /* FLEX, not grid, and that is the whole trick. A percentage in
     `grid-auto-columns` has no definite inline size to resolve against inside a
     `grid-auto-flow:column` scroller, so it silently resolves to zero —
     measured, `minmax(0,calc((100% - 20px)/2.4))` collapsed all sixteen tiles to
     9px of min-content. A flex item's percentage basis resolves against the
     flex container's CONTENT BOX, which is the 296px visible box and is
     definite whatever the scroll width is. So 2.4 tiles fit at any width in
     this band with no hard-coded gutter arithmetic to go stale. */
  .ggn-presets{display:flex}
  .ggn-preset{flex:0 0 calc((100% - 20px) / 2.4)}
}
/* THE DESKTOP CASE: WRAP, DO NOT SCROLL.
   The rail above is right for a phone, where sixteen 142px tiles cannot share a
   296px box on any terms. It is wrong from 981px up, and it was measured wrong:
   at 1280 AND at 1440 the strip is 2422px inside a 1142px box, so 1280px of it
   sits behind a 34px fade with SEVEN of the sixteen visible. The eight a desktop
   visitor never reaches without a gesture are Sunset, Lagoon, Grape, Citrus,
   Twilight, Aurora, Bloom and Ocean, and the page was holding ~300px of empty
   ground under the card while it hid them.
   The fix vocabulary in the `.rail-overflow` note says to wrap first where the
   item set is small and discrete, and to keep the three layered affordances for
   rails where wrapping is genuinely not on the table. Sixteen named presets is
   small and discrete, and a preset strip is a BROWSE-AND-COMPARE surface: the
   reader is working out what is on offer, which is the one job scrolling is
   worst at. Mobbin, web, searched for the opposite pattern and it is not there
   - Mural, Aave and Discord all lay a named-swatch library out WRAPPED and
   entire, and none of the four references puts one in a horizontal scroller.
   `auto-fill` at a 132px minimum lands on eight columns in the 1142px card, so
   the sixteen become two rows of eight at 1280 and at 1440, and three rows
   lower in the band - the whole library, no gesture, for one extra row of
   height that the page already had spare.
   The rail affordances are switched OFF here rather than on below, because
   `.rail-overflow` is on the element in the markup: same shape as the
   `.plb-toolbar-group.rail-overflow` opt-out at >=961px. */
@media(min-width:981px){
  .ggn-presets{display:grid;grid-auto-flow:row;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(132px,1fr));padding-bottom:0}
  .ggn-presets.rail-overflow{overflow:visible;scroll-snap-type:none;animation:none;-webkit-mask-image:none;mask-image:none}
  .ggn-preset{scroll-snap-align:none}
}
.ggn-import:focus-visible,.ggn-css:focus-visible,.ggn-btn:focus-visible,.ggn-seg-btn:focus-visible,.ggn-copy:focus-visible,.ggn-stop-x:focus-visible,.ggn-angle-input:focus-visible,.ggn-stop-pos input:focus-visible,.ggn-stop-hex:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--ggn-accent);outline-offset:2px}
@media(max-width:980px){
  .ggn-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}
  /* Single column: drop the height cap entirely — the panel flows with the page
     and must never become a nested scroll area. The two-up field layout moves
     onto the body, which is now the fields' direct parent. */
  .ggn-panel-slot{position:static}
  .ggn-panel{position:static;display:block}
  .ggn-panel-body{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:0 var(--s-5);overflow:visible;padding-right:0;margin-right:0}
  .ggn-field:last-child{grid-column:1/-1}
}
@media(max-width:768px){
  .ggn{padding-top:var(--s-5)}
  .ggn-head{align-items:flex-start}
  .ggn-head-actions{width:100%;justify-content:flex-start}
  .ggn-status{grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr))}
  .ggn-status span:nth-child(3){border-left:0;border-top:1px solid var(--ggn-line)}
  .ggn-status span:nth-child(4){border-top:1px solid var(--ggn-line)}
  .ggn-panel-body{display:block}
  .ggn-stops{grid-template-columns:1fr}
}
@media(max-width:640px){
  /* The global mobile form rule lifts every number input to 16px with 11px/14px
     padding so iOS can't zoom on focus. Keep the 16px — that's the point — but
     drop the wide padding and widen the field, or a 3-digit angle clips inside
     the compact Angle row. */
  input.ggn-angle-input{width:46px;padding-inline:0}
}
@media(max-width:480px){
  .ggn-title{font-size:36px}
  .ggn-status{grid-template-columns:1fr}
  .ggn-status span+span,.ggn-status span:nth-child(3){border-left:0;border-top:1px solid var(--ggn-line)}
  .ggn-stage{padding:8px}
  .ggn-preview{aspect-ratio:4/3}
  .ggn-bar{margin-inline:6px}
  .ggn-block{padding:16px}
  .ggn-block-head{align-items:flex-start;flex-direction:column}
  .ggn-block-actions{width:100%;flex-wrap:wrap}
  .ggn-block-actions .ggn-btn{flex:1}
  .ggn-stop{display:grid;grid-template-columns:20px 32px minmax(0,1fr) auto auto}
  .ggn-stop-pos{grid-column:3/4}
  .ggn-stop-lock{grid-column:4}
  .ggn-stop-x{grid-column:5}
}

/* ── Gradient Library (/discover/gradients) ──
   The masthead and result head are the shared Discover components (dgh-/drh-);
   only the richer filter toolbar and the gradient cards are local to this page. */
.grg-wrap{padding-bottom:88px}
/* Toolbar, grid, card frame, foot and empty state now come from the shared
   Library language (.lbry-, below). What is left here is what is genuinely
   specific to a gradient: the swatch, and the three controls that hang off it.
   The type badge that used to be `.grg-pill` — hover-only — is now the shared
   `.lbry-card-badge` and is visible at rest.

   THE SWATCH IS LANDSCAPE, and it is `height:196px` rather than a ratio for one
   reason: that is exactly what `.pgal-stripes` on /discover/palettes is. These
   two galleries were extracted into one shared browse language (.lbry-) because
   they had solved the same problem twice and disagreed on every part of it —
   and then they still disagreed about the only thing a browser actually looks
   at, the shape of the tile. `aspect-ratio:3/4` was never overridden at any
   breakpoint, so at 390px one portrait tile was 358x477 — 57% of the screen for
   a single gradient — and the page ran to 57,839px against the palette
   gallery's landscape rows. Two sibling galleries sharing one browse language
   browsed completely differently.
   A ratio cannot fix it, because a ratio is what caused it: the tile has to
   stop growing when the column does. A fixed height is also what every colour
   gallery worth copying does — Adobe Color, Relume's palette library and
   Semrush's brand kits are all landscape and dense, and none of them uses a
   portrait swatch. Measured, shipped rule against the old one injected back:
   57,839px → 29,706px at 390px, and 31,330px → 15,330px at 834px. Half the
   page, for the same number of gradients. */
.grg-swatch{display:block;height:196px;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:inherit;box-shadow:var(--shadow-s)}
.grg-swatch:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--accent);outline-offset:3px}
.grg-like{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:var(--radius-pill);border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.35);background:rgba(10,10,14,.42);color:#fff;cursor:pointer;-webkit-backdrop-filter:blur(6px);backdrop-filter:blur(6px);transition:opacity var(--dur-1) var(--ease-standard),transform var(--dur-2) var(--ease-standard),background var(--dur-1) var(--ease-standard),color var(--dur-1) var(--ease-standard),border-color var(--dur-1) var(--ease-standard)}
.grg-like:hover{background:rgba(10,10,14,.62)}
.grg-like:focus-visible{outline:2px solid #fff;outline-offset:2px}
.grg-like.is-liked{color:#fb7185;background:rgba(10,10,14,.5);border-color:rgba(251,113,133,.6)}
@media(hover:hover){
  .grg-like{opacity:0;transform:translateY(-2px)}
  .grg-card:hover .grg-like,.grg-like:focus-visible,.grg-like.is-liked{opacity:1;transform:none}
}
.grg-copy{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:var(--fs-micro);font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.04em;color:var(--t1);background:var(--bg-2);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius-s);padding:6px 11px;cursor:pointer;transition:color var(--dur-1) var(--ease-standard),border-color var(--dur-1) var(--ease-standard),background var(--dur-1) var(--ease-standard)}
.grg-copy:hover{color:var(--t0);border-color:var(--bh);background:var(--bg-3)}
.grg-open{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:5px;font-family:var(--font);font-size:var(--fs-micro);font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.02em;color:var(--accent-strong);background:none;border:none;padding:6px 4px;cursor:pointer;text-decoration:none;transition:gap var(--dur-2) var(--ease-standard),color var(--dur-1) var(--ease-standard)}
.grg-open:hover{gap:8px;color:var(--brand-soft)}
.grg-empty{margin-top:8px}
/* Submissions queue — deliberately styled as a LIST, not as gallery cards, so
   a pending gradient never reads as one that has been published. */
.grg-queue{margin-top:32px;padding:16px;border:1px dashed var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius-l);background:var(--bg-1)}
.grg-queue-note{margin:6px 0 14px;max-width:70ch;font-size:var(--fs-caption);color:var(--t2)}
.grg-queue-list{list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:8px}
.grg-queue-item{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:12px;padding:10px 12px;background:var(--card);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius)}
.grg-queue-swatch{flex:0 0 auto;width:56px;height:32px;border-radius:var(--radius-s);border:1px solid var(--border)}
.grg-queue-id{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;min-width:0;flex:1 1 auto}
.grg-queue-id strong{font-size:13px;font-weight:650;color:var(--t0);overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;white-space:nowrap}
.grg-queue-status{flex:0 0 auto;padding:4px 9px;border-radius:var(--radius-pill);background:var(--warn);color:var(--warn-fg);font-family:var(--mono);font-size:var(--fs-micro);font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.03em}
@media(max-width:640px){
  .grg-queue-item{flex-wrap:wrap}
  .grg-queue-id{flex:1 1 100%;order:2}
  .grg-queue-status{order:1}
}
/* Gradient preview at the top of the submit-for-review modal. */
.ggn-submit-preview{height:88px;margin-bottom:var(--s-4);border-radius:var(--radius);border:1px solid var(--border)}
@media(max-width:820px){
  .grg-search{flex:1 1 100%}
  /* Keeps its own row, but keeps wrapping too — the nowrap scroller this used
     to impose put 4 of 8 mood filters outside the box at 320px and 3 of 8 at
     390px, with no affordance. Same fault as S4 on the shared .pl-chips. */
  .grg-chips{flex:1 1 100%}
}
/* M6 (responsive-audit-2026-08): every gradient name and meta line truncated
   between 450 and 579px. Measured across the 100-card library: at 450px, 78 of
   100 names and 100 of 100 meta lines were cut, e.g. "Sunset Blaze" needing 86px
   and getting 47.

   THE AUDIT'S DIAGNOSIS IS WRONG, and the difference is worth 250% of the page
   height. It blamed the 200px grid minimum below ("well below the ~245px the
   card footer actually needs") and proposed raising it to the desktop 280px.
   That does clear the truncation, because it stops there being two columns at
   all until 608px — measured, the grid goes from 17,849px to 64,658px tall at
   450px, +262%, to browse the same 100 cards one enormous 619px card at a time.

   The actual cause is in the footer, not the grid. `.grg-actions` is
   `flex-shrink:0`, so Copy CSS + Open take a fixed ~142px however narrow the
   card is, and `.grg-id` gets what is left — 47px in a 201px column. The name is
   not being denied width by the column; it is being denied width by the action
   row beside it. Stacking the two clears every truncation at every width for
   +11% card height (330px to 368px at 450px), and keeps two columns.

   Below 450px the grid is already a single 288-408px column and nothing was
   truncated there, so this is not needed for correctness — it is applied to the
   whole ≤640px band anyway so that a phone card has ONE footer shape rather than
   changing arrangement at an invisible seam, and it gives the two actions a
   full-width row, which is the friendlier touch target of the two layouts. */
@media(max-width:640px){
  .grg-grid{grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(200px,1fr));gap:28px 16px}
  .grg-toolbar{gap:10px;margin-bottom:24px}
  .grg-foot{flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;gap:8px}
  .grg-actions{justify-content:space-between}
}

/* ─── Typography tools — shared chrome (typ-) ───────────────────────────────
   The Font Gallery, Font Pair and Type Scale tools all read the same Google
   Fonts catalogue, so they share one set of network-state and font-picking
   classes rather than each inventing its own. Tool-specific layout stays in
   the per-tool blocks (fg- / fpr- / tsc-). */
.typ-loading{display:flex;min-height:320px;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;justify-content:center;gap:10px;text-align:center;color:var(--t2);padding:var(--s-5)}
.typ-loading strong{font-size:var(--fs-body);color:var(--t0)}
.typ-loading span{max-width:46ch;font-size:var(--fs-caption);line-height:1.55}
.typ-notice{display:flex;gap:12px;align-items:flex-start;margin:0 0 var(--s-5);padding:14px 16px;border-radius:var(--radius);background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--warn) 10%,var(--bg-0));border:1px solid color-mix(in srgb,var(--warn) 35%,transparent)}
.typ-notice>svg{color:var(--warn);flex-shrink:0;margin-top:1px}
.typ-notice>div{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;min-width:0}
.typ-notice strong{font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:var(--t0)}
.typ-notice span{font-size:12.5px;color:var(--t1);line-height:1.55}
/* TWO TINTS WERE STACKING HERE. .typ-notice is an amber warn tint; this button
   then laid --brand-bg (a translucent blue) on top of it, so the label sat on a
   muddy composite of both — measured #d6d7d6 in light and #30332f in dark, for
   4.04:1 and 4.11:1 against 12px/600 text that needs 4.5.
   Neither tint was visible to the contrast walker before its parser learned to
   read color-mix(), which is why this shipped. A solid --card surface removes
   the stack entirely and is the better button anyway: a secondary action inside
   a warning notice should read as a raised control, not as a second wash of
   colour over the first. Measured after: 5.84:1 light, 5.57:1 dark. */
.typ-notice-retry{align-self:flex-start;margin-top:6px;min-height:34px;padding:6px 14px;font-size:12px;font-weight:600;border-radius:var(--radius-s);border:1px solid var(--brand);background:var(--card);color:var(--accent-strong);cursor:pointer;transition:background var(--dur-1) var(--ease-standard),color var(--dur-1) var(--ease-standard)}
.typ-notice-retry:hover:not(:disabled){background:var(--accent-strong);color:var(--accent-fg)}
.typ-notice-retry:disabled{opacity:.6;cursor:progress}
.typ-notice-retry:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--brand);outline-offset:2px}
.typ-hint{margin:0 0 20px;font-size:11.5px;line-height:1.5;color:var(--t2)}
/* Font picker — a specimen you can see, not a name you have to recall.
   The trigger shows the CURRENT family rendered in itself and opens the browse
   dialog. It replaced a native <select>, which renders every option in the UI
   font and so could never show a single one of the faces on offer. */
.typ-picker{display:flex;flex-direction:column;margin-bottom:14px}
/* Specimen on its own row, above the name — NOT beside it. Side by side, a
   104px face box clipped "Handgloves" mid-word, which reads as a broken
   control rather than a preview. Full width the specimen gets ~290px in the
   narrowest panel that hosts this, which is enough for a real word. */
.typ-picker-trigger{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr) auto;gap:3px 12px;align-items:center;width:100%;padding:10px 12px 10px 14px;text-align:left;background:var(--inp);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius-l);cursor:pointer;transition:border-color var(--dur-1) var(--ease-standard),box-shadow var(--dur-1) var(--ease-standard),background var(--dur-1) var(--ease-standard)}
.typ-picker-trigger:hover:not(:disabled){border-color:var(--bh);background:var(--hvr)}
.typ-picker-trigger:focus-visible{outline:none;border-color:var(--brand);box-shadow:0 0 0 3px var(--brand-bg)}
.typ-picker-trigger:disabled{opacity:.55;cursor:not-allowed}
/* Fixed height: the specimen changes family on every pick, and letting it size
   the row would bounce the whole panel each time you chose something. */
.typ-picker-face{grid-column:1;grid-row:1;display:flex;align-items:center;min-width:0;height:34px;overflow:hidden;font-family:var(--fbd-ff,var(--font));font-weight:var(--fbd-fw,600);font-size:26px;line-height:1.05;letter-spacing:-.02em;color:var(--t0);white-space:nowrap}
.typ-picker-id{grid-column:1;grid-row:2;display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:7px;min-width:0}
.typ-picker-name{flex-shrink:0;max-width:60%;font-size:12px;font-weight:650;color:var(--t0);white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis}
.typ-picker-meta{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10px;color:var(--t2);text-transform:capitalize;white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis}
.typ-picker-cue{grid-column:2;grid-row:1/span 2;align-self:center;flex-shrink:0;padding:6px 11px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:var(--accent-strong);background:var(--brand-bg);border-radius:var(--radius-pill)}
/* Kept: Font Pair's "reload the catalogue" recovery button still uses it. */
.typ-picker-clear{margin-top:6px;min-height:32px;padding:6px 12px;font-family:var(--font);font-size:11px;font-weight:600;color:var(--t1);background:var(--bg-2);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius-s);cursor:pointer;transition:border-color var(--t),background var(--t),color var(--t)}
.typ-picker-clear:hover{border-color:var(--bh);background:var(--hvr);color:var(--t0)}
.typ-picker-clear:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--brand);outline-offset:2px}

/* Browse dialog */
.fbd-overlay{position:fixed;inset:0;z-index:120;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;padding:clamp(12px,4vw,40px);background:rgba(8,8,12,.58);-webkit-backdrop-filter:blur(6px);backdrop-filter:blur(6px)}
.fbd-dialog{display:flex;flex-direction:column;width:min(1080px,100%);max-height:min(880px,92vh);padding:clamp(18px,3vw,28px);background:var(--bg-0);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius-2xl);box-shadow:0 40px 120px rgba(6,6,12,.42)}
.fbd-dialog:focus{outline:none}
.fbd-head{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:16px;margin-bottom:16px}
.fbd-head h2{margin:0;font-size:clamp(19px,2.4vw,24px);font-weight:650;letter-spacing:-.02em;color:var(--t0)}
.fbd-close{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;width:36px;height:36px;flex-shrink:0;color:var(--t2);background:var(--bg-2);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius-pill);cursor:pointer;transition:color var(--dur-1) var(--ease-standard),border-color var(--dur-1) var(--ease-standard)}
.fbd-close:hover{color:var(--t0);border-color:var(--bh)}
.fbd-close:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--brand);outline-offset:2px}
/* Not sticky inside the dialog: the dialog's own body is the scroll container,
   and a sticky bar inside a scrolling flex child pins to the wrong box. */
.fbd-toolbar{position:static;margin-bottom:12px}
.fbd-count{margin:0 0 14px;font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10px;letter-spacing:.11em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--t2)}
.fbd-body{flex:1;min-height:0;overflow-y:auto;padding-right:4px}
.fbd-grid{--lbry-col-min:190px}
.fbd-card .lbry-card-meta{text-transform:capitalize}
/* `container-type:inline-size` is here so the specimen can size itself off the
   TILE rather than off the viewport — see .fbd-sample for why that matters. */
.fbd-tile{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;width:100%;height:104px;padding:10px 14px;background:var(--card);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius-l);cursor:pointer;container-type:inline-size;transition:border-color var(--dur-1) var(--ease-standard),background var(--dur-1) var(--ease-standard)}
.fbd-tile:hover{border-color:var(--bh)}
.fbd-tile:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--brand);outline-offset:2px}
.fbd-card.is-selected .fbd-tile{border-color:var(--brand);background:var(--brand-bg);box-shadow:inset 0 0 0 1px var(--brand)}
/* THE SPECIMEN IS THE CONTROL, SO IT MUST NEVER BE TRUNCATED.
   At 390px this read "Hand…", "Handgl…", "Handg…" — a different amount of a
   different word per family, so the tiles stopped being comparable at exactly
   the width where comparing them is hardest. A fixed 30px inside a column that
   drops to 150px cannot fit ten characters, and the ellipsis quietly hid that.

   `cqw` sizes the word off the TILE, so the specimen shrinks with its own box
   instead of overflowing it, at any column count and any viewport. Note cqw
   resolves against the container's CONTENT box, so the tile's 14px side padding
   is already discounted — calibrating against the border box gets this wrong by
   exactly that much, which is how the first attempt landed on the floor.

   15cqw is measured, not guessed: "Handgloves" renders between 4.8× and 6.0×
   its font-size across the five categories (serif narrowest, some display and
   mono faces widest), so the widest face needs 15cqw × 6.0 = 90% of the box and
   keeps ~10% headroom. The 30px ceiling keeps every tile from ~228px up
   identical to what shipped; the floor never binds at any shipped column count.

   The ellipsis stays as the backstop. It is genuinely last-resort now rather
   than the normal case, but it is NOT claimed to be unreachable: "Handgloves"
   is 6.3× its font-size in the widest face measured across the loaded corpus,
   and nothing stops family 1,947 being wider. If it ever fires again the
   answer is this ratio, not a shorter word — a specimen trimmed to fit is the
   fault being fixed here, not a fix for it. */
.fbd-sample{max-width:100%;overflow:hidden;font-family:var(--fbd-ff,var(--font));font-weight:var(--fbd-fw,600);font-size:clamp(17px,15cqw,30px);line-height:1.15;letter-spacing:-.02em;color:var(--t0);white-space:nowrap;text-overflow:ellipsis}
/* A tile whose face has not registered yet shows nothing rather than the
   fallback: the tile exists to be judged, and a fallback pretending to be the
   family is the one thing it must never do. */
.fbd-sample--pending{color:transparent;background:linear-gradient(90deg,var(--bg-2),var(--bg-3),var(--bg-2));background-size:200% 100%;border-radius:var(--radius-xs);animation:fg-shimmer 1.4s linear infinite}
.fbd-more{display:flex;justify-content:center;padding:18px 0 4px}
.fbd-empty{margin-bottom:8px}

/* ── The per-family detail view, and the two tabs the founder asked for ──────
   Three fixed rows and ONE scroller: head, tabs, scrolling panel, pinned
   action. #305 found the Font Gallery's specimen dialog 1,763px tall in a 900px
   viewport with every action below the fold, and noted that adding tabs is
   exactly how that comes back. It does not come back here because the dialog
   never grows — .fbd-detail is a flex column inside the same capped
   .fbd-dialog, so a longer panel scrolls the panel, not the dialog. */
.fbd-info{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;width:30px;height:30px;color:var(--t1);background:var(--bg-1);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius-pill);cursor:pointer;transition:color var(--dur-1) var(--ease-standard),border-color var(--dur-1) var(--ease-standard)}
.fbd-info:hover{color:var(--t0);border-color:var(--bh)}
.fbd-info:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--brand);outline-offset:2px}
.fbd-detail{display:flex;flex-direction:column;flex:1;min-height:0}
.fbd-detail-head{display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:14px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:12px}
.fbd-back{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:6px;padding:6px 12px 6px 8px;font:inherit;font-size:12px;color:var(--t1);background:var(--bg-2);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius-pill);cursor:pointer;transition:color var(--dur-1) var(--ease-standard),border-color var(--dur-1) var(--ease-standard)}
.fbd-back:hover{color:var(--t0);border-color:var(--bh)}
.fbd-back:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--brand);outline-offset:2px}
/* The name is the specimen — it is set in the family it names, which is the
   same contract the tiles keep. */
.fbd-detail-name{margin:0;min-width:0;font-family:var(--fbd-ff,var(--font));font-weight:var(--fbd-fw,700);font-size:clamp(21px,3.2vw,30px);line-height:1.1;letter-spacing:-.02em;color:var(--t0)}
.fdx-tabs{display:flex;gap:4px;flex-shrink:0;margin-bottom:14px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)}
.fdx-tab{position:relative;padding:9px 14px;font:inherit;font-size:13px;font-weight:550;color:var(--t2);background:none;border:0;border-radius:var(--radius-s) var(--radius-s) 0 0;cursor:pointer;transition:color var(--dur-1) var(--ease-standard)}
.fdx-tab:hover{color:var(--t0)}
.fdx-tab:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--brand);outline-offset:-2px}
.fdx-tab--on{color:var(--t0)}
/* Sits ON the 1px border rather than above it, so the pair reads as one edge
   and not as a rule with a second rule stacked on it. */
.fdx-tab--on::after{content:"";position:absolute;left:10px;right:10px;bottom:-1px;height:2px;background:var(--brand);border-radius:2px 2px 0 0}
.fbd-detail-body{flex:1;min-height:0;overflow-y:auto;padding-right:4px}
.fbd-detail-actions{flex-shrink:0;margin-top:14px;padding-top:14px;border-top:1px solid var(--border)}
.fbd-use{width:100%;padding:12px 18px;font:inherit;font-size:14px;font-weight:600;color:#fff;background:var(--brand);border:1px solid var(--brand);border-radius:var(--radius);cursor:pointer}
.fbd-use:hover{filter:brightness(1.06)}
.fbd-use:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--brand);outline-offset:2px}
.fbd-cuts{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:14px;margin:0;padding:0;list-style:none}
.fbd-cuts li{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:3px;padding-bottom:13px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)}
.fbd-cuts li:last-child{border-bottom:0;padding-bottom:0}
.fbd-cut-line{font-family:var(--fbd-ff,var(--font));font-weight:var(--fbd-fw,400);font-size:clamp(15px,2.4vw,20px);line-height:1.32;color:var(--t0)}
.fbd-cut-label{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10px;letter-spacing:.09em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--t2)}

/* ── FontDossier: the About and Examples panels, shared by both font popups ──
   Prefixed fdx- rather than fbd- because these two panels are NOT the browse
   dialog's — they are the shared component the Font Gallery specimen dialog
   uses too, and a fbd- name would have made the next person move them. */
/* Radius declared at REST, not on :focus-visible — see the note on the
   global :focus-visible rule near the top of this file. This element paints
   no background and no border of its own, so the radius is visually inert
   here; it exists so the focus ring traces a shape the element actually
   has, instead of the ring's own declaration changing the shape to match
   itself. */
.fdx-panel{border-radius:var(--radius-s)}
.fdx-panel:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--brand);outline-offset:2px}
/* A definition list, not a card grid. Nine facts in nine equally-weighted
   rounded boxes is the "every idea in a card" failure — these are rows of a
   spec, they have a natural label/value shape, and dl says so semantically. */
.fdx-facts{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,auto) minmax(0,1fr);gap:0;margin:0}
.fdx-fact{display:contents}
.fdx-fact dt{grid-column:1;padding:9px 20px 9px 0;font-size:12px;font-weight:550;color:var(--t2);white-space:nowrap;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)}
.fdx-fact dd{grid-column:2;margin:0;padding:9px 0;font-size:13px;line-height:1.45;color:var(--t0);border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)}
.fdx-fact:last-child dt,.fdx-fact:last-child dd{border-bottom:0}
.fdx-note{margin-top:18px;padding:14px 16px;background:var(--bg-2);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius)}
.fdx-note h4{margin:0 0 6px;font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.01em;color:var(--t0)}
.fdx-note p{margin:0;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:var(--t1)}
.fdx-source{margin:14px 0 0;font-size:11.5px;line-height:1.5;color:var(--t2)}
.fdx-source--lead{margin:0 0 16px}

/* Each example is the family doing a job, with the question it answers stated
   above it. Aave's brand-typography section states a role under each face
   rather than adjectives; the caption here is that idea turned into a question,
   because an example a reader cannot interpret teaches nothing.

   THIS IS NOW A LIBRARY, NOT A LAYOUT. The scenes are chosen per family in
   src/utils/fontScenes.js and any one family renders three or four of the
   sixteen below, so these rules are grouped by the role that reaches them and
   several groups never co-occur on screen.

   WHAT IS SPECIMEN AND WHAT IS CHROME, decided once here: type set in
   the --fdx-ff family is the one under examination and is the thing being
   judged (written as a bare token name, not as a var() call: design-tokens
   .test.js scans this stylesheet without stripping comments, so a var() in
   prose reads to it as a real fallback-less usage and fails the build);
   the small labels around it (.fdx-ex-kicker, .fdx-ex-runglabel, the gutter
   numerals) stay in --font or --mono so a reader can always tell which letters
   are the evidence. The one deliberate exception is .fdx-ex-overline, which is
   set in the family: on a poster the date line really is part of the
   composition, and how a display face behaves at 12px is worth seeing.

   THREE WEIGHT CUSTOM PROPERTIES, all snapped upstream to cuts the family
   really ships: --fdx-bw body, --fdx-mw mid, --fdx-hw bold. On a single-weight
   family they all resolve to the same number and every scene renders flat,
   which is the truth about that family. The fallbacks in var() are only for a
   scene rendered outside the panel. */
.fdx-examples{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:22px}
.fdx-ex-head h4{margin:0;font-size:12px;font-weight:600;color:var(--t0)}
.fdx-ex-head p{margin:2px 0 10px;font-size:11.5px;line-height:1.45;color:var(--t2)}
.fdx-ex-body{padding:18px;background:var(--bg-1);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius);font-family:var(--fdx-ff,var(--font));color:var(--t0);overflow-wrap:break-word}
.fdx-ex-body p,.fdx-ex-body h5,.fdx-ex-body li,.fdx-ex-body td,.fdx-ex-body th,.fdx-ex-body dt,.fdx-ex-body dd,.fdx-ex-body blockquote{font-family:inherit}
/* Anything that can outgrow the dialog scrolls in its OWN box. A 64-character
   code line and a four-column table must never make the panel — or the page —
   scroll sideways, which is the regression #350 fixed on the specimen and this
   block is wide enough to reintroduce. */
.fdx-ex-scroll{max-width:100%;overflow-x:auto}

/* Shared chrome. --t2 not --t3 throughout: --t3 measures 4.29:1 on dark, which
   clears nothing under 24px, and every label here is under 24px. */
.fdx-ex-kicker{display:block;margin-bottom:8px;font-family:var(--mono);font-size:9.5px;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--t2)}
.fdx-ex-overline{display:block;font-weight:var(--fdx-mw,500);font-size:12px;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--t2)}
/* p.fdx-ex-verdict, not .fdx-ex-verdict. This is the app COMMENTING on the
   specimen, so it must be in the UI font by the rule stated at the top of
   this block — but .fdx-ex-body p {font-family:inherit} is more specific than
   a lone class and was winning, so the sentence explaining that a script is
   hard to read at length was itself set in the script, at 12px. Caught in a
   screenshot, not by any structural check: every assertion about fonts here
   passed while this was wrong. */
.fdx-ex-body p.fdx-ex-verdict{margin:12px 0 0;padding-top:12px;font-family:var(--font);font-size:12px;line-height:1.5;color:var(--t2);border-top:1px solid var(--border)}

/* ── Display: poster, masthead, title card, and the same face small ───────── */
.fdx-ex-poster{text-align:center}
.fdx-ex-postertitle{margin:10px 0;font-weight:var(--fdx-hw,700);font-size:clamp(30px,6.5vw,48px);line-height:.98;letter-spacing:-.02em}
.fdx-ex-posterfoot{display:block;font-weight:var(--fdx-bw,400);font-size:13px;line-height:1.4;color:var(--t1)}
.fdx-ex-mastname{margin:0;font-weight:var(--fdx-hw,700);font-size:clamp(26px,5.5vw,38px);line-height:1.05;letter-spacing:-.02em}
.fdx-ex-mastdeck{margin:8px 0 0;padding-top:10px;font-weight:var(--fdx-bw,400);font-size:14px;line-height:1.45;color:var(--t1);border-top:1px solid var(--border)}
.fdx-ex-titlecard{text-align:center}
.fdx-ex-title{margin:10px 0 0;font-weight:var(--fdx-hw,700);font-size:clamp(20px,4vw,30px);line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase}
/* 13px is the claim the caption makes, so it is a literal 13px and not a
   clamp — a scene whose whole argument is a size must actually be that size. */
.fdx-ex-smalltext{margin:0;font-weight:var(--fdx-bw,400);font-size:13px;line-height:1.55;color:var(--t1)}

/* ── Editorial: article, reading column, pull quote ───────────────────────── */
.fdx-ex-editorial h5{margin:0 0 8px;font-weight:var(--fdx-hw,700);font-size:clamp(19px,3vw,25px);line-height:1.16;letter-spacing:-.015em}
.fdx-ex-lede{margin:0 0 10px;font-weight:var(--fdx-bw,400);font-size:15px;line-height:1.5;color:var(--t1)}
.fdx-ex-editorial p:last-child{margin:0;font-weight:var(--fdx-bw,400);font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--t1)}
/* A REAL MEASURE, which is the entire point of this scene: 34em at 14px is
   roughly 62 characters, inside the 45–75 the copy itself talks about. Without
   the ch cap the column is however wide the dialog is, and the scene would be
   asking a question it had just prevented the reader from answering. */
.fdx-ex-column p{max-width:34em;margin:0 0 11px;font-weight:var(--fdx-bw,400);font-size:14px;line-height:1.62;color:var(--t1)}
.fdx-ex-column p:last-child{margin-bottom:0}
.fdx-ex-pullquote blockquote{margin:0;padding-left:14px;border-left:2px solid var(--bh)}
.fdx-ex-pullquote blockquote p{margin:0;font-weight:var(--fdx-mw,500);font-size:clamp(17px,2.8vw,22px);line-height:1.35;letter-spacing:-.01em}
.fdx-ex-attrib{display:block;margin-top:10px;padding-left:16px;font-family:var(--font);font-size:11.5px;color:var(--t2)}

/* ── Slab: signage ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
.fdx-ex-signrow{display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:14px}
.fdx-ex-signnum{font-weight:var(--fdx-hw,700);font-size:clamp(34px,7vw,52px);line-height:1}
.fdx-ex-signname{font-weight:var(--fdx-mw,500);font-size:clamp(19px,3.4vw,26px);letter-spacing:-.01em}
.fdx-ex-signnote{margin:12px 0 0;padding-top:10px;font-weight:var(--fdx-bw,400);font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:var(--t1);border-top:1px solid var(--border)}

/* ── Sans: interface, dense data, a form under load ───────────────────────── */
.fdx-ex-bar{display:flex;gap:4px;margin-bottom:14px;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)}
.fdx-ex-tab{padding:5px 10px;font-weight:var(--fdx-bw,400);font-size:13px;color:var(--t2);border-radius:var(--radius-s)}
.fdx-ex-tab--on{font-weight:var(--fdx-hw,600);color:var(--t0);background:var(--bg-2)}
.fdx-ex-uinote{margin:0 0 12px;font-weight:var(--fdx-bw,400);font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:var(--t1)}
.fdx-ex-uirow{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px}
.fdx-ex-btn{display:block;padding:9px 14px;font-weight:var(--fdx-hw,600);font-size:13px;text-align:center;color:var(--t0);background:var(--bg-2);border:1px solid var(--bh);border-radius:var(--radius-s)}
.fdx-ex-btn--quiet{font-weight:var(--fdx-bw,400);color:var(--t2);background:none}
.fdx-ex-table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:13px}
.fdx-ex-table th{padding:0 0 7px;font-weight:var(--fdx-hw,600);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase;text-align:left;color:var(--t2);border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);white-space:nowrap}
.fdx-ex-table td{padding:8px 0;font-weight:var(--fdx-bw,400);color:var(--t1);border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);white-space:nowrap}
.fdx-ex-table td:first-child{color:var(--t0)}
.fdx-ex-table th:not(:first-child),.fdx-ex-table td:not(:first-child){padding-left:18px;text-align:right}
.fdx-ex-table tr:last-child td{border-bottom:0}
.fdx-ex-form{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px}
.fdx-ex-label{font-weight:var(--fdx-hw,600);font-size:12px;color:var(--t0)}
.fdx-ex-label--2{margin-top:12px}
.fdx-ex-input{padding:9px 11px;font-weight:var(--fdx-bw,400);font-size:13px;color:var(--t0);background:var(--bg-2);border:1px solid var(--bh);border-radius:var(--radius-s)}
.fdx-ex-input--bad{border-color:var(--err-strong)}
.fdx-ex-hint{font-weight:var(--fdx-bw,400);font-size:11.5px;line-height:1.45;color:var(--t2)}
.fdx-ex-error{font-weight:var(--fdx-mw,500);font-size:11.5px;line-height:1.45;color:var(--err-strong)}

/* ── Monospace: code, terminal, figures ───────────────────────────────────── */
/* white-space:pre and NO tab stops, NO text-align — if the columns land, the
   face did it. That is the only thing these two scenes are asking. */
.fdx-ex-codelines{counter-reset:fdxln;margin:0;padding:0;list-style:none;font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.7}
.fdx-ex-codelines li{counter-increment:fdxln;display:flex;gap:14px;white-space:pre;font-weight:var(--fdx-bw,400);color:var(--t1)}
.fdx-ex-codelines li::before{content:counter(fdxln);flex:none;width:1.6em;text-align:right;color:var(--t2);-webkit-user-select:none;user-select:none}
.fdx-ex-shell{margin:0;font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.7;font-weight:var(--fdx-bw,400);color:var(--t1);white-space:pre}
.fdx-ex-figlist{margin:0}
.fdx-ex-figrow{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:24px;padding:7px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)}
.fdx-ex-figrow:last-child{border-bottom:0}
.fdx-ex-figrow dt{font-weight:var(--fdx-bw,400);font-size:13.5px;color:var(--t1)}
/* NO font-variant-numeric. Forcing tabular figures onto a face that has none
   would answer the question this scene exists to ask. */
.fdx-ex-figrow dd{margin:0;font-weight:var(--fdx-mw,500);font-size:15px;color:var(--t0)}

/* ── Script: invitation, signature, and where it stops working ────────────── */
.fdx-ex-invite{text-align:center}
.fdx-ex-invite .fdx-ex-overline{letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:none}
.fdx-ex-invitetitle{margin:12px 0;font-weight:var(--fdx-hw,700);font-size:clamp(24px,5vw,38px);line-height:1.2}
.fdx-ex-invitefoot{display:block;font-weight:var(--fdx-bw,400);font-size:14px;line-height:1.45;color:var(--t1)}
.fdx-ex-sign{margin:0;font-weight:var(--fdx-bw,400);font-size:clamp(24px,5vw,38px);line-height:1.3}
.fdx-ex-signfoot{display:block;margin-top:10px;font-family:var(--font);font-size:11.5px;color:var(--t2)}

/* ── Symbols: the marks, because letters are not what this family draws ───── */
/* anywhere, not break-word: a barcode family renders each character as a wide
   block, so a 26-character run is thousands of pixels and has no spaces in it
   for a normal break to use. */
.fdx-ex-glyphrow{margin:0 0 10px;font-weight:var(--fdx-bw,400);font-size:clamp(17px,3.4vw,24px);line-height:1.6;overflow-wrap:anywhere}
.fdx-ex-glyphrow:last-child{margin-bottom:0}

/* ── The ladder, drawn only when the family ships three or more cuts ──────── */
.fdx-ex-ladder{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:12px}
.fdx-ex-rung{display:flex;align-items:baseline;justify-content:space-between;gap:16px;min-width:0}
.fdx-ex-rungline{margin:0;min-width:0;font-size:clamp(19px,3.6vw,27px);line-height:1.2;overflow-wrap:anywhere}
.fdx-ex-runglabel{flex:none;font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10px;letter-spacing:.06em;color:var(--t2)}

/* ── The outbound search, worded as a search ──────────────────────────────── */
.fdx-fiu-note{margin:18px 0 0;padding-top:14px;font-size:11.5px;line-height:1.55;color:var(--t2);border-top:1px solid var(--border)}
/* Resting underline, not a hover one — founder direction 2026-09-02 rules out
   hover-only affordances on touch, and the footer attribution link settled the
   same question the same way. 70% of currentColor rather than a fixed token,
   which is what measured clear of 1.4.11 there. */
/* Radius at REST, not on :focus-visible — see the note on the global
   :focus-visible rule near the top of this file. An inline link paints no box,
   so 2px is inert here and exists only so the ring has a shape to trace. */
.fdx-fiu{border-radius:2px;color:var(--t1);text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:color-mix(in srgb,currentColor 70%,transparent);text-underline-offset:2px}
.fdx-fiu:hover{color:var(--t0)}
.fdx-fiu:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--brand);outline-offset:2px}
@media(max-width:560px){
  .fbd-dialog{max-height:96vh;padding:16px}
  /* 200px, not 150px. At 150 a phone got two ~150px tiles, and a 122px content
     box cannot hold ten characters of a display face at a judgeable size — the
     specimen either ellipsised (what shipped) or shrank under 17px (what the
     first fix did). 200 makes a 390px phone one full-width tile, where the face
     renders at the full 30px ceiling, and still gives two tiles from ~470px up.
     Fewer families per screen is the right trade for a picker whose entire
     premise is that you choose by LOOKING: search and the category tray are how
     a 1,852-family corpus is actually navigated, not scrolling. */
  .fbd-grid{--lbry-col-min:200px}
  /* NO `width` here. This is the third time this control has been given a fixed
     face box and the second time it has had to be taken away again — the design
     note above `.typ-picker-trigger` records the first, where a 104px box cut
     "Handgloves" mid-word and read as a broken control rather than a preview.
     `width:78px` did it again and worse: at 390px the specimen showed "Handglo"
     with the o sliced down the middle and ~200px of empty trigger to the right
     of the cut. The base rule already has everything needed to stay inside the
     row — `min-width:0`, `overflow:hidden`, and a `minmax(0,1fr)` grid track —
     so the width was never load-bearing, only harmful. The font-size drop is
     kept; that is what actually buys room on a phone. */
  .typ-picker-face{font-size:22px}
  .typ-picker-cue{display:none}
}

/* ─── Font Pair (/fontpairs) — fpr- ─────────────────────────────────────────
   The specimen and every suggestion card take their two families and two
   weights from CSS custom properties set on the element, so the live preview
   never needs an inline style attribute. */
.fpr-page{min-width:0;padding-bottom:var(--s-4)}
.fpr-hero{margin-bottom:var(--s-5)}
.fpr-hero-copy{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr) minmax(380px,560px);gap:var(--s-8);align-items:end}
.fpr-hero h1{margin:0;font-family:var(--display);font-size:clamp(38px,5vw,64px);font-weight:500;line-height:1.04;letter-spacing:-.035em;color:var(--t0)}
.fpr-hero-copy>p{max-width:600px;margin:0 0 4px;font-size:15px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--t1)}
.fpr-status{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,minmax(0,1fr));margin-bottom:var(--s-5);border-block:1px solid var(--border)}
.fpr-status span{display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:6px;min-width:0;padding:12px 16px;font-size:11px;color:var(--t2)}
.fpr-status span+span{border-left:1px solid var(--border)}
.fpr-status strong{min-width:0;font-family:var(--mono);font-size:12px;color:var(--t0);overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;white-space:nowrap}
.fpr-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr) 340px;gap:var(--s-5);align-items:start;min-width:0}
.fpr-grid>*{min-width:0}
/* ── THE CONTROLS COME FIRST IN THE DOM NOW ──────────────────────────────────
   Font Pair is a workbench: you choose two families, then read what they do
   together. The markup said the opposite. "Read the pairing" was first and
   "Choose the pair" was a sibling after it, which was invisible at ≥1344px
   (where the sidebar sits alongside) and severe below it — the two-column
   layout only starts at 1344px, so at 1280, a completely ordinary laptop
   width, the page stacked and the font pickers landed 1,930px BELOW the
   specimen, after the suggestions and after the CSS export. Changing a font
   meant scrolling past everything the font was supposed to affect. The step
   numbers recorded the inversion rather than fixing it: they ran 01, 03, 04
   down the main card with 02 stranded in the sidebar.

   The backlog left this open as "a RESTRUCTURE of the card ... explicitly left
   as a design decision", and the decision taken here is to REORDER THE DOM
   rather than renumber to match a broken order. So the source order is now
   choose → read → suggestions → handoff, the numbers are 01/02/03/04 in that
   order at every width, and the sidebar is put back on the right by grid
   placement in the one band that has a sidebar. Tab order follows the DOM, so
   the keyboard now reaches the controls before their output — which is the
   right order for a workbench regardless of which column things land in.

   Scoped to the two-column band ONLY: below it the grid is a single column and
   an explicit `grid-column:2` would invent a phantom second track. */
@media(min-width:1344px){
  .fpr-output{grid-column:1;grid-row:1}
  .fpr-config{grid-column:2;grid-row:1}
}
.fpr-panel{display:flex;flex-direction:column;min-width:0;padding:24px;background:var(--card);overflow:hidden}
.fpr-panel.card:hover{border-color:var(--border)}
/* Sticky config column. The sticky offset is a custom property so the premium
   restyle further down can move the panel without the height maths drifting
   away from it. A sticky box with no ceiling is only as tall as its content, so
   at 1440x900 the "choose the pair" panel measured 906px against 808px of
   usable viewport and clipped its own "Build a scale from this pair" hand-off —
   .fpr-panel{overflow:hidden} meant the overhang could not even be scrolled to.
   Bound it to the viewport and give it its own scroller. overflow-y must sit
   here, not on .fpr-panel: same specificity, later in the file. dvh, not vh, so
   a mobile URL bar cannot push the bottom out of reach. */
/* The vh line is a REQUIRED fallback, not a duplicate. An engine that does not
